stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n320:| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n347:### Host Dispatch\n383:## Surfaces\n451:### Where the asynchrony goes\n455:a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n457:boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n462:This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n469:## Cursors\n474:  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n481:  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n482:  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n489:  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n490:  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n491:  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n532:## Reading\n540:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n541:| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n542:| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n543:| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n545:| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n554:## Writing\n560:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n561:| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n562:| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n563:| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n571:not here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n577:## Close\n581:is also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n599:`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n618:- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\n619:- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead\n620:  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.\n627:## Concurrency\n640:`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a\n650:## Retention and Gaps\n661:**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n679:### Complete history\n739:## Composition\n747:A forwarder between streams is a single step (not a callback loop) over the\n748:transport-agnostic stream algebra. Because the stream never blocks, cadence is\n759:## Explicitly Absent\n781:- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n782:- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n790:## Open Decisions\n807:### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n840:say when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n847:### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n892:### OD-3. A cursor binds to the logical stream but is shaped by one transport\n945:### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n985:### OD-5. \"No operation waits\" binds the seven operations and nothing above them\n988:\"unimplementable on most of the targets\" and that a callback contract \"inverts\n1002:  must wait, which `dao.jing.md` records honestly: `connect-content!` is \"a\n1013:  no socket, atom, promise, callback, or scheduler\": `request-get`, `step`,\n1022:locally, on a host that cannot wait: a browser or Dart agent querying a remote\n1027:adopt the callback shape, which reintroduces inverted control into every\n1044:   > now, including \"not yet\", over streams the caller supplied. A waiting\n1045:   > call or a callback may wrap that step as **host policy**, named as such\n1060:this is the existing park and wait-set path; in library code it is the shape\n1067:written on the JVM is written against the waiting shape, and each one is a\n1071:### Recorded, not proposed\n1090:  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n1:# Design: `dao.await`\n3:## Summary\n5:`dao.await` provides familiar async-style syntax for writing sequential stream\n8:It does not introduce a new interpreter, scheduler, continuation model, or\n12:(await/go <forms>)\n16:-> Yin VM owns continuations and parks on stream effects\n23:callbacks, promises, or blocking calls as its core mechanism.\n25:`dao.await` is a syntax and process-construction layer. It is not `dao.flow`.\n28:## Homomorphism with `core.async`\n30:Structurally, `dao.await` is a *homomorphism* of `core.async`. It is a structure-preserving projection from the Clojure host domain into the Yin/Datom domain. The relationships between operations are preserved, allowing a developer's mental model to map perfectly from one to the other, even though the execution substrate is completely different:\n34:- **The Read Operation:** Parking on a channel callback maps to Yin parking on a `:stream/next` effect\n36:- **The Scheduler:** Host Thread Pool / Event Loop maps to the `dao.runtime` ready queue and its polling wait set\n38:Because the structure is preserved, developers gain the proven ergonomics of Communicating Sequential Processes (CSP) without violating the system invariant that **runtime state must be data**. While `core.async` compiles code into an opaque host-level state machine, `dao.await` compiles code into pure datoms interpreted by the Yin VM, leaving the entire execution state explicitly queryable and serializable.\n40:## Layer Boundaries\n44:- `dao.stream` exposes non-blocking operations — `cursor`, `next`,\n48:  continuations and must not gain await-specific control flow.\n49:- `yin.vm` interprets semantic datoms, owns continuations, and handles parking\n52:- `dao.await` supplies user-facing syntax and starts or describes a Yin-backed\n58:dao.await/go targets Universal AST, not dao.runtime.\n61:Any continuation produced by an await block is a Yin VM continuation. It is not\n62:a `dao.await` continuation and not a `dao.runtime` continuation.\n64:## Public API\n66:V1 should add a `dao.await` namespace with these public forms or functions:\n69:(await/go body...)\n70:(await/cursor stream)\n71:(await/<! cursor)\n72:(await/>! stream value)\n78:(require '[dao.await :as await])\n80:(await/go\n81:  (let [c (await/cursor in)\n82:        x (await/<! c)]\n83:    (await/>! out [:seen x])\n87:V1 uses explicit cursors. Do not implement implicit cursor reuse in the first\n90:### `await/go`\n92:`await/go` accepts Clojure forms and delegates form compilation to\n99:(await/go expr1 expr2 expr3)\n111:`await/go` should not directly interpret body forms. It may analyze body forms\n114:should be valid inside `await/go`, subject to the available runtime module\n117:The return value of `await/go` should be an explicit process value or task\n123:{:type :dao.await/process\n134:### Host Lexical Capture\n136:`await/go` must bridge host lexical scope into the Yin environment. A naive\n150:  (await/go\n151:    (await/>! out my-val)))\n154:In this example, the await program must receive the value of host binding\n164:     '(await/>! out my-val))\n167:`go*` must then seed the Yin program environment or store so that those symbols\n169:should follow existing Yin VM environment/store conventions. Do not use hidden\n172:Free-variable analysis must treat locally bound symbols inside the await body\n185:(await/go {:env {'my-val my-val\n187:  (await/>! out my-val))\n193:### `await/cursor`\n195:`await/cursor` creates a stream cursor in the Yin program.\n197:It should lower through the same semantics as existing stream cursor support:\n200:(await/cursor s)\n203:is equivalent to the existing stream cursor operation, `cursor` on\n207:(stream/cursor s)\n211:the module system, or it may lower directly to a `:stream/cursor` AST node if\n215:### `await/<!`\n217:`await/<!` reads the next value from an explicit cursor.\n220:(await/<! c)\n232:If the stream has a value at the cursor position, Yin returns that value and\n233:stores the successor cursor `next` returned. If the read returns `blocked`, Yin\n234:parks the current continuation using existing stream effect handling. Nothing\n235:wakes the parked task: it resumes when the next poll of `dao.runtime`'s\n236:wait set returns a value for its cursor. A value written after the park is\n240:invent a new end-of-stream contract in `dao.await`.\n242:### `await/>!`\n244:`await/>!` writes a value to a stream.\n247:(await/>! out value)\n259:current continuation. Nothing wakes it: `dao.runtime` re-attempts the append\n260:on every poll of the wait set, and the task resumes when a poll's append\n264:## Compilation Strategy\n270:resolver and Yang compiler paths. Do not add a new `:await/*` node family unless\n273:### Compile-Time vs Runtime Compilation\n275:Avoid compiling the same await body every time the host code executes.\n293:time on the host platform, the `await/go` macro should emit precompiled\n306:### Route 1: Module Aliases\n308:Teach the Yin module environment that the await names produce existing stream\n312:await/cursor -> {:effect :stream/cursor ...}\n313:await/<!     -> {:effect :stream/next ...}\n314:await/>!     -> {:effect :stream/put ...}\n317:With this route, `yang.clojure` can compile await calls as ordinary\n322: :operator {:type :variable, :name 'await/<!}\n328:This route minimizes changes to `yang.clojure`, but couples await semantics to\n329:runtime module resolution. Use it if the module system already cleanly supports\n330:aliasing `await/cursor`, `await/<!`, and `await/>!` to existing stream effect\n333:### Route 2: Yang Special Lowering\n339:(await/cursor s)  -> {:type :stream/cursor, :source <compiled-s>}\n340:(await/<! c)      -> {:type :stream/next, :source <compiled-c>}\n341:(await/>! s v)    -> {:type :stream/put, :target <compiled-s>, :val <compiled-v>}\n344:Do not add a new `:await/*` node family to Universal AST unless there is a\n345:concrete need. The existing stream AST nodes already express the behavior.\n347:This route treats await operations as compiler intrinsics over existing stream\n350:already has `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, and `:stream/put` node types.\n353:`dao.await`, with a short note explaining why the selected route has less\n356:## Process Construction\n358:`await/go` must produce or start a Yin-backed process from the compiled AST.\n366:- `dao.runtime` task maps with a `:resume` function.\n368:V1 should choose the smallest integration that allows tests to drive an await\n402:the source forms are compiled by Yang, not interpreted by `dao.await`, and host\n405:## Runtime Semantics\n407:The await block runs as a Yin program.\n411:- `await/cursor` creates a cursor value in the VM store.\n412:- `await/<!` executes the existing `:stream/next` effect.\n413:- `await/>!` executes the existing `:stream/put` effect.\n418:If a read is blocked or a write is full:\n420:- Yin parks the current continuation.\n423:- `dao.runtime` records the task in the wait set. There are no\n424:  transport-local waiters: the polling wait set is the only mechanism.\n427:  resolves it moves it to the ready queue. A stream that is written to,\n432:- `dao.runtime` invokes the task's `:resume` function with the polled\n434:- The VM restores the Yin continuation and continues evaluating the await body.\n436:`dao.await` must not implement these steps itself. It relies on the existing\n439:## Non-Goals\n446:- A new await interpreter with its own program counter, local environment, or\n447:  continuation data structure.\n448:- A new `dao.runtime` continuation abstraction.\n449:- Implicit cursor creation or cursor reuse.\n455:## Tests\n459:### Yang / AST Tests\n461:Cover compilation of the public await forms:\n463:- `(await/cursor s)` compiles to either an await module application or a\n464:  `:stream/cursor` AST node, depending on the selected route.\n465:- `(await/<! c)` compiles to either an await module application or a\n467:- `(await/>! s 42)` compiles to either an await module application or a\n469:- `(await/go expr1 expr2)` compiles the body as `(do expr1 expr2)` and preserves\n471:- A host lexical binding referenced inside `await/go` is captured and resolves\n473:- Symbols bound inside the await body are not captured as free variables.\n474:- Quoted symbols inside the await body are data and are not captured.\n475:- Repeated execution of the same `await/go` body uses precompiled AST/datoms or\n478:### VM Behavior Tests\n484:- Prefilled input stream: `await/<!` returns the first value.\n485:- Output write: `await/>!` appends to the output stream.\n487:- Empty read: the VM blocks, then resumes on the next wait-set poll after\n490:  thereafter, the VM blocks and the wait set's retry append resolves it. (The\n493:  contracts without await-specific translation.\n495:### Runtime Boundary Tests\n499:- Runtime queue entries are ordinary task maps with `:resume`.\n500:- No await-specific continuation fields are required by `dao.runtime`.\n503:## Future Work\n508:(await/<! stream)\n511:where `dao.await` creates and reuses an implicit cursor inside the lexical\n512:`await/go` body.\n514:That feature requires a clear cursor policy:\n516:- whether repeated reads from the same stream expression share a cursor,\n518:- how generated cursor bindings are named in the emitted forms,\n519:- how cursor state appears in semantic datoms and provenance.\n521:Do not add this policy until explicit-cursor await programs are implemented and\n524:## Implementation Checklist\n527:2. Add `dao.await` namespace.\n528:3. Register await module functions or add Yang special lowering.\n529:4. Implement `await/go` as a thin wrapper around `yang.clojure` compilation and\n531:5. Drive await programs through an existing VM implementation in tests.\n532:6. Verify blocking read/write behavior through `dao.runtime` and wait-set\n533:   polling: a parked task is resumed by the poll that resolves it, never by a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '210,382p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '450,585p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '800,875p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '980,1095p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '1,75p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '215,270p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '405,445p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '210,382p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '450,585p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '800,875p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '980,1095p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '1,75p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '215,270p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '405,445p'\"","aggregated_output":"   210\t| `yin.repl`'s host shell — one tick owner per host: clj `poll-loop!` (`repl.cljc:194`, `Thread/sleep`), cljs `run-node!` (`repl.cljc:267`, `setInterval`), cljd `run-dart!` (`repl.cljc:352`, `Timer.periodic`) — all stepping `step-all` (`repl.cljc:93`) | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:21` (`tick-millis`, 25 ms); `repl-step` `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:536`, whose `poll-remote` (`:480`) → `adapter/poll-responses` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:202`) → `rpc/poll!`; `serve/step` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:684`) | Local input drain, the remote session's response medium, and the served endpoint's whole serving tick — every 25 ms whether or not anything moved | Multiplexed (one tick owner over all of it) | This is the fixed-timer cadence W3's driver replaces: the tick owner becomes a driver with `:poll-ms`/`:budget`, and the append-side readers (the per-host read-line handlers, `request-stop!`) become `nudge!` callers. The endpoint's multiplexing itself moves to `waitset/check` via the serving row above |\n   211\t\n   212\tLoops found beyond the expected rows:\n   213\t\n   214\t| Loop (caller) | Where | Waits on | Scope | What a waitset adoption changes |\n   215\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   216\t| `dao.jing.remote`'s `connect-content!` establishment wait — JVM-only host policy, named as such by `dao.stream.md` OD-5 | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:733-747`, stepping `await-established-step` (`:363`, one `rpc/poll!` at `:373`) per round, then `Thread/sleep` | The attachment's `/established` lifecycle element on one response medium, to a deadline | Single-stream | A driver plus waitset would replace the sleep-poll, and a nudge would end the sleep at establishment instead of at the next interval. Deliberately out of scope: this is the documented blocking host policy |\n   217\t| `dao.jing.remote`'s `call!` request wait | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:587-636`, stepping `call-step` (`:294`, `rpc/poll!` at `:308`) per round, then `Thread/sleep` under a deadline | One awaited call's completion; interruption is a first-class exit | Single-stream (one awaited call per thread) | As above — one sleeping JVM thread per in-flight call is precisely the idle cost the nudge halves |\n   218\t| `dao.postgraphics.terminal`'s viewer pump `step-until-blocked` — driven by the demo, web, and flutter hosts after renders or frames (`src/cljd/dao/postgraphics/flutter.cljd:339`, `src/cljs/dao/postgraphics/web.cljs:106`) | `src/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:169`, over `step` (`:125`, at most one frame read per call) | Its one frame medium; a bounded drain-until-blocked, after which the host retries at its next opportunity | Single-stream | Nothing should change. Same drain-on-progress shape as the GUI pump; no idle loop exists to replace |\n   219\t| One-shot catch-up drains: `dao.space.query/snapshot` and `datomworld.demo.continuation_transport` | `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:299`; `src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_transport.cljc:121,153` | Catch one cursor up to `blocked`/`end` once, for a relation value or a demo handoff | Single-stream | Nothing. A snapshot is a read that ends, not a waiter; there is no cadence across rounds to hand a driver |\n   220\t\n   221\tVerified to contain no loop of their own, so no row: `dao.stream.observe/step`\n   222\t(`observe.cljc:58`), `dao.stream.forward/forward-step` (`forward.cljc:67`),\n   223\t`dao.jing.remote.step`'s client (`step.cljc:492` composes `rpc/poll!` into a\n   224\tpure step), `yin.repl.connect/observe` (`connect.cljc:525`, one `stream/next`\n   225\tat `:542` per call, stepped by the shell's tick owner), and `dao.await`, which\n   226\tonly emits `:stream/next`/`:stream/put` effects (`await.cljc:66`) — its\n   227\twaiting is the engine row above. Every expected row was found in the source;\n   228\tnone was invented.\n   229\t\n   230\t## Phase W1 — The sweep\n   231\t\n   232\t`src/cljc/dao/stream/waitset.cljc`, pure, requiring only `dao.stream`:\n   233\t\n   234\t- `empty-waitset`, `park` (add an entry), `check` as specified above.\n   235\t- Tests iterate `dao.stream/outcomes-next` and `outcomes-append` and pin\n   236\t  the classification of every declared outcome — exactly `blocked` and\n   237\t  `full` wait — so a future contract outcome fails this suite instead of\n   238\t  falling into a default branch. A keyword outside the declared sets is\n   239\t  asserted terminal.\n   240\t- Tests for the two preserved properties: shared cursor-ref wakes\n   241\t  co-waiters on distinct values in one round; a scripted `full`-once\n   242\t  handle shows exactly one append per poll with the resolved writer in\n   243\t  `:woken` and no polled state discarded.\n   244\t\n   245\tDeliverable: suite green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd.\n   246\t\n   247\t## Phase W2 — The engine becomes a consumer\n   248\t\n   249\tReplace `augment-wait-entry`, `poll-wait-entry`, and `check-wait-set` in\n   250\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` with `waitset/check` plus a store-resolver\n   251\tand the woken-to-ready-queue disposition (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`\n   252\tstays the engine's). The run loop, park semantics, and terminal-outcome\n   253\traising are unchanged.\n   254\t\n   255\tDeliverable: the full `yin.vm` suite — engine, walker, FFI, macro, parity —\n   256\tgreen on all three hosts **without test edits**, and `grep` confirming the\n   257\tthree inline functions are gone. Any test that had to change is a defect in\n   258\tW1, not in the test.\n   259\t\n   260\t## Phase W3 — Cadence containers (`dao.stream.waitset.driver`)\n   261\t\n   262\tOne namespace name, three host files, the R2 shape with explicit state:\n   263\t`src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`,\n   264\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs`,\n   265\t`src/cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd`.\n   266\t\n   267\t- **clj**: `make-driver` returns `{:waitset … :queue (LinkedBlockingQueue.)\n   268\t  :poll-ms … :budget …}` held in an atom the host creates. `run-loop!`\n   269\t  blocks on the queue with the `:poll-ms` timeout — external entries and\n   270\t  `nudge!` wakes it immediately, timeout means the wait set is due a\n   271\t  round — then sweeps and dispatches `:woken` through the host's\n   272\t  disposition. This is `select` with the wakeup pipe; the sweep is the\n   273\t  syscall.\n   274\t- **cljs/cljd**: `schedule-work!` enqueues and schedules one microtask;\n   275\t  `run-pending!` sweeps and, if the wait set is non-empty, arms exactly\n   276\t  one timer at `:poll-ms` (cancel-and-rearm; Node timers `unref`ed), so an\n   277\t  idle composition costs one timer, zero busy loops.\n   278\t- `nudge!` on every host: enqueues a wake token (clj) or schedules the\n   279\t  pending run immediately (cljs/cljd).\n   280\t- Tests port the deleted R2 driver tests' intents over v2 ring buffers and\n   281\t  a host-created container: internal work drains, a parked wait set keeps\n   282\t  being polled without external work, `nudge!` beats the timer, repeated\n   283\t  nudges coalesce, an idle driver stays alive at backoff ceiling.\n   284\t\n   285\tDeliverable: three drivers and suites on their hosts. No production\n   286\tconsumer is switched in this phase, and the plan says so rather than\n   287\tinventing one: the drivers exist because a sweep without cadence has no\n   288\tanswer to \"what calls `check` again\" on any host, and because `a99f246f`\n   289\tdeleted the only answer that existed.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Phase W4 — Consumer adoption\n   292\t\n   293\tMigrate the multiplexed consumers W0 identified — expected: the GUI event\n   294\tpump and `dao.stream.rpc`'s serving loop; `dao.jing` if its materializer\n   295\tholds more than one waiter. Single-stream consumers stay on `observe/step`\n   296\tand `observer/run-on-stream`: the waitset is for hosts holding many\n   297\twaiters, and converting a one-stream loop onto it is the over-adoption\n   298\tfailure mode this phase exists to avoid. Each adoption deletes the loop it\n   299\treplaces; a consumer adopted without deleting anything has been wrapped,\n   300\tnot migrated, and is reverted.\n   301\t\n   302\tDeliverable: each adopted consumer's suite green on all three hosts with\n   303\tits private poll loop gone.\n   304\t\n   305\t## Phase W5 — Design prose and stale references\n   306\t\n   307\t- The `dao.stream.observe` namespace docstring's reference to\n   308\t  \"`dao.runtime`'s wait set\" names `dao.stream.waitset`.\n   309\t- [`dao.await.md`](./dao.await.md) describes resumption as \"the scheduler\n   310\t  polls each parked entry\" — the sentence gains the library's name and the\n   311\t  nudge rule (the VM parks; the library sweeps; the driver keeps cadence).\n   312\t- [`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) *The readiness extension* gains one\n   313\t  sentence pointing at this library as the standing answer to *What it\n   314\t  costs*: poll-based, substrate-untouched, and the reason no consumer\n   315\t  needs the reserved extension to sleep between rounds.\n   316\t\n   317\tDeliverable: the three docs updated; no other prose touched.\n   318\t\n   319\t## Divergence register\n   320\t\n   321\tEvery place the library deliberately differs from what it replaces, with\n   322\tthe reason.\n   323\t\n   324\t| Prior form | `dao.stream.waitset` | Why |\n   325\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   326\t| Engine entries carry VM registers and ride the machine relation | Entries are `{:reason :stream-ref :cursor-ref}` data; disposition is host-owned | A VM entry is a continuation; the library multiplexes readiness. Register-bearing entries would drag the VM into every consumer |\n   327\t| Engine resolves refs against its `:store` internally (`engine.cljc:266`) | Resolver is a parameter; `check` returns a new store | The deferred decision, adopted: a second consumer class appeared (W0) |\n   328\t| `dao.runtime` had `:resume`, a ready queue, and task semantics | None of the three | Task scheduling is why the old runtime was coupled to its consumers; readiness multiplexing is the reusable core. A library with `:resume` is a scheduler, and schedulers are per-host policy |\n   329\t| Deleted R2 drivers polled on a fixed timer only | `nudge!` wakes an idle driver immediately | The timer-only shape pays full `:poll-ms` latency on externally-appended data; the nudge halves the cost of the select analogy without touching the substrate |\n   330\t| `run-once`/`run-loop` combined ready work and polling | `check` polls; the host's disposition runs woken work | The R1 split, restated: a polled state is never discarded, and the sweep owns no downstream work to drop |\n   331\t| Unknown wait `:reason` kept waiting (old runtime default) | Unchanged | An entry the library cannot poll is not the library's to resolve; it stays where the host put it |\n   332\t\n   333\t## Host matrix\n   334\t\n   335\tW1 and W2 are pure `.cljc` over `dao.stream` and have no host branch. W3 is\n   336\tthree host files by construction. Every phase green on clj, cljs (Node) and\n   337\tcljd; per the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.stream.waitset-test` and\n   338\t`Testing dao.stream.waitset.driver-test` appear in the Node output rather\n   339\tthan assuming discovery. The cljd test lane regenerates `test/cljd-out/`;\n   340\tonly one process may own it at a time.\n   341\t\n   342\t## Boundary of this plan\n   343\t\n   344\t**Not in this plan, by design:**\n   345\t\n   346\t- Any change to `dao.stream`, its protocols, outcome sets, or transports.\n   347\t  Zero diff is an end condition.\n   348\t- The contract's reserved readiness extension. The library is the\n   349\t  demonstration that polling plus a nudge suffices; if it ever is not,\n   350\t  the extension lands in transports and this library consumes it —\n   351\t  additively, per the contract.\n   352\t- `dao.await` and the VM's park/resume relation. W2 swaps the engine's\n   353\t  sweep implementation only.\n   354\t- Fairness, priorities, indexes (see *Decisions*), and any queue\n   355\t  interpreter restoring destructive take — an interpreter above the\n   356\t  stream, per the runtime plan's boundary, not a wait-set feature.\n   357\t- Consumers that stay single-stream on `observe/step`. They are not\n   358\t  technical debt; they are the correct shape for one stream and one\n   359\t  consumer.\n   360\t\n   361\t## End condition\n   362\t\n   363\tComplete when all of the following hold on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd:\n   364\t\n   365\t- `dao.stream.waitset` requires only `dao.stream`, classifies every\n   366\t  declared outcome with exactly `blocked`/`full` waiting, preserves the\n   367\t  shared-cursor and no-dropped-poll laws, and its suite proves each by\n   368\t  construction.\n   369\t- `yin.vm.engine` contains no inline wait-set functions and its suites\n   370\t  pass unmodified.\n   371\t- `dao.stream.waitset.driver` exists per host with explicit state,\n   372\t  configurable cadence, budget, and `nudge!`, over v2 fixtures only.\n   373\t- The W4 consumers run the library and contain no private multiplexed\n   374\t  poll loops; the single-stream consumers are recorded as deliberately\n   375\t  unchanged in W0's table.\n   376\t- `git diff <base> -- src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/`\n   377\t  shows no transport changes beyond the new `waitset/` subtree.\n   378\t- The W5 prose names the library, and no doc or docstring names the\n   379\t  deleted `dao.runtime` as a live namespace.\n   450\t\n   451\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   452\t\n   453\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   454\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   455\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   456\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   457\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   458\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   459\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   460\twhen it chooses.\n   461\t\n   462\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   463\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   464\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   465\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   466\tstated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,\n   467\tand its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.\n   468\t\n   469\t## Cursors\n   470\t\n   471\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   472\t\n   473\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   474\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   475\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   476\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   477\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   478\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   479\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   480\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   481\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   482\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   483\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   484\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   485\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   486\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   487\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   488\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   489\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   490\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   491\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   492\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   493\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   494\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   495\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   496\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   497\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   498\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   499\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   500\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   501\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   502\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   503\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   504\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   505\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   506\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   507\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   508\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   509\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   510\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   511\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   512\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   513\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   514\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   515\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   516\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   517\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   518\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   519\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   520\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   521\t  descriptor envelope.\n   522\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   523\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   524\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   525\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   526\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   527\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   528\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   529\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   530\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   531\t\n   532\t## Reading\n   533\t\n   534\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   535\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   536\tanswer.\n   537\t\n   538\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   539\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   540\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   541\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   542\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   543\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t\n   548\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   549\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   550\tother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\n   551\ttake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\n   552\treader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   553\t\n   554\t## Writing\n   555\t\n   556\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   557\t\n   558\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   559\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   560\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   561\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   562\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   563\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   565\t\n   566\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   567\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   568\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   569\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   570\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   571\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   572\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   573\t\n   574\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   575\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   576\t\n   577\t## Close\n   578\t\n   579\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n   580\tother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\n   581\tis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n   582\t\n   583\t`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n   584\tresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\n   585\tA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\n   800\t\n   801\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   802\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   803\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   804\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   805\tcontract's own operations.\n   806\t\n   807\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   808\t\n   809\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   810\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   811\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   812\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   813\t\n   814\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   815\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   816\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   817\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   818\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   819\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   820\t\n   821\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   822\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   823\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   824\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   825\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   826\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   827\t\n   828\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   829\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   830\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   831\t\n   832\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   833\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   834\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   835\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   836\t> assembly.\n   837\t\n   838\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   839\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   840\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   841\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   842\t\n   843\t**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n   844\t\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n   845\tacross all of them.\n   846\t\n   847\t### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n   848\t\n   849\t**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\n   850\tfailed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\n   851\ta definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\n   852\tthe value is in the sequence.\n   853\t\n   854\t**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\n   855\tare single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\n   856\tits writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\n   857\ttransport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\n   858\t\n   859\t**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\n   860\tlog replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\n   861\ta third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\n   862\tagainst the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\n   863\tnothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\n   864\t\n   865\tTwo related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\n   866\twhere the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\n   867\tof the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\n   868\tcontract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\n   869\t`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\n   870\t`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\n   871\tway to learn any of the three later.\n   872\t\n   873\t**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\n   874\t\n   875\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   980\t\n   981\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport ships as \"complete\", gains\n   982\tconsumers that depend on that word, and can then never reclaim space without\n   983\tbreaking them.\n   984\t\n   985\t### OD-5. \"No operation waits\" binds the seven operations and nothing above them\n   986\t\n   987\t**The assumption.** *The IO Model* argues that a blocking contract is\n   988\t\"unimplementable on most of the targets\" and that a callback contract \"inverts\n   989\tcontrol on all of them\", and concludes that non-blocking polling is the only\n   990\tmodel that means the same thing on clj, cljs, and cljd. The argument is about\n   991\thosts, not about streams, so it applies with equal force to any interface an\n   992\tinterpreter publishes. The contract nevertheless binds only its own seven\n   993\toperations. It assumes the layers above will inherit the model without being\n   994\ttold to.\n   995\t\n   996\t**Evidence.** Content lookup, the one random-access service in the system, is\n   997\tcurrently published in three shapes:\n   998\t\n   999\t- **Waiting.** `dao.jing/get` is `(get handle address not-found) -> value`, and\n  1000\t  `materialize!` returns an address \"only after the backend reports success\".\n  1001\t  Over a local store this is one in-memory or file read. Over a remote store it\n  1002\t  must wait, which `dao.jing.md` records honestly: `connect-content!` is \"a\n  1003\t  blocking driver as host policy… a JVM thread polls and sleeps\", and both\n  1004\t  halves of `dao.jing.remote` are JVM-only. Nine call sites depend on this\n  1005\t  shape: three in `dao.space.index` (index walks and manifest reads), four in\n  1006\t  `dao.data.btree.storage`, two in `yin.vm.content`.\n  1007\t- **Callback.** `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async` takes `on-ok` and\n  1008\t  `on-err` or returns a Promise / Future / CompletableFuture, tracking\n  1009\t  completion in atoms, over an async content handle\n  1010\t  (`:get-content-async-fn`). This is the second of the three IO models, the\n  1011\t  one this document rejects by name, and it sits one layer above the contract.\n  1012\t- **Stepped.** `dao.jing.remote.step` is \"a pure function over explicit data…\n  1013\t  no socket, atom, promise, callback, or scheduler\": `request-get`, `step`,\n  1014\t  `abandon` over `dao.stream.rpc`. This is the third model, and the only one of\n  1015\t  the three shapes that is the same on every host.\n  1016\t\n  1017\t`dao.jing.md` already lists the consequences as deferred items (*The content\n  1018\twrite path as an effect stream*, *Async hydration*). What is undecided is not\n  1019\twhether the problem exists but which shape is the interface.\n  1020\t\n  1021\t**What breaks.** Any interpreter that must reach content it does not hold\n  1022\tlocally, on a host that cannot wait: a browser or Dart agent querying a remote\n  1023\t`dao.space` index; a VM loading code by address from another node\n  1024\t(`yin.vm.content`); a game client fetching meshes or textures by address.\n  1025\tToday each has two options. It can hydrate the whole reachable graph before\n  1026\treading, which turns a selective index read into a full download. Or it can\n  1027\tadopt the callback shape, which reintroduces inverted control into every\n  1028\tcaller above it, the viral cost *Why this model and not the other two*\n  1029\tdescribes.\n  1030\t\n  1031\tThe general form matters more than this instance. Every future service that\n  1032\tanswers a question (a lookup, a query against a remote peer, a lease grant, a\n  1033\tname resolution) faces the same three shapes. Without a rule each will choose\n  1034\tby local convenience, and the convenient choice on the JVM is the one that\n  1035\tdoes not port.\n  1036\t\n  1037\t**Proposed resolution.** One rule, stated here because this is where the\n  1038\targument for it lives, and one consequence, stated in `dao.jing.md`:\n  1039\t\n  1040\t1. Add to *Composition*:\n  1041\t\n  1042\t   > The IO model is inherited. An interpreter's portable interface is\n  1043\t   > expressed the way this contract's is: a step that returns what is true\n  1044\t   > now, including \"not yet\", over streams the caller supplied. A waiting\n  1045\t   > call or a callback may wrap that step as **host policy**, named as such\n  1046\t   > and confined to the hosts that can afford it, exactly as\n  1047\t   > `connect-content!` wraps the stepped client. It is never the interface\n  1048\t   > another interpreter is written against.\n  1049\t\n  1050\t2. In `dao.jing.md`, name the stepped request/response pair as the\n  1051\t   agent-facing interface to content, and reclassify the synchronous handle\n  1052\t   (`:get-content-fn`) as what it already is in practice: the store\n  1053\t   interpreter's boundary adapter onto its own medium, plus a cache readers may\n  1054\t   consult. The handle map does not change. What changes is which of the three\n  1055\t   shapes new code is allowed to depend on.\n  1056\t\n  1057\tTwo costs follow and should be accepted with open eyes. The tree walks in\n  1058\t`dao.space.index` and `dao.data.btree.storage` become steppable: fetch, receive\n  1059\t\"not yet\", return state, resume when the response is observed. Inside `yin.vm`\n  1060\tthis is the existing park and wait-set path; in library code it is the shape\n  1061\t`forward-step` and `rpc/poll!` already have. And `hydrate-async` is either\n  1062\trewritten as a step or explicitly labelled host policy. The hydration cache\n  1063\titself survives unchanged: prefetching a graph so that a walk never misses is a\n  1064\tlegitimate optimization of a stepped interface, not an alternative to one.\n  1065\t\n  1066\t**Cost if deferred.** The nine call sites grow. Every new consumer of content\n  1067\twritten on the JVM is written against the waiting shape, and each one is a\n  1068\tseparate port when the first browser or Dart agent needs to read a remote\n  1069\tindex.\n  1070\t\n  1071\t### Recorded, not proposed\n  1072\t\n  1073\tThese came out of the same review. Each is reachable by addition under the\n  1074\trules the contract already has, so none needs a decision now. They are listed\n  1075\tso that a later plan does not rediscover them as surprises.\n  1076\t\n  1077\t- **A writer does not learn where its value landed.** `append!`'s `ok` carries\n  1078\t  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\n  1079\t  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\n  1080\t  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\n  1081\t  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\n  1082\t  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\n  1083\t- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\n  1084\t  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\n  1085\t  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n  1086\t  elements live in the payload, above the stream. The obligation on this layer\n  1087\t  is only the one it already meets: identity is compared structurally and never\n  1088\t  parsed.\n  1089\t- **Readiness.** Polling is O(n) in streams observed (*What it costs*). The\n  1090\t  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n  1091\t  the cost in the runtime without it.\n  1092\t- **Granularity.** An element is a whole value; there is no byte offset within\n  1093\t  one and no chunked element. Large media travel as content addresses in the\n  1094\t  payload, resolved through `dao.jing`.\n     1\t# Design: `dao.await`\n     2\t\n     3\t## Summary\n     4\t\n     5\t`dao.await` provides familiar async-style syntax for writing sequential stream\n     6\tprograms that run on the existing Yin VM and Dao runtime stack.\n     7\t\n     8\tIt does not introduce a new interpreter, scheduler, continuation model, or\n     9\tblocking stream API. The implementation reuses the existing pipeline:\n    10\t\n    11\t```text\n    12\t(await/go <forms>)\n    13\t-> yang.clojure compiles forms to Universal AST\n    14\t-> yin.vm/ast->datoms emits semantic datoms\n    15\t-> Yin VM executes semantic datoms\n    16\t-> Yin VM owns continuations and parks on stream effects\n    17\t-> dao.runtime schedules resumable VM task maps\n    18\t-> dao.stream provides non-blocking, outcome-returning transport\n    19\t```\n    20\t\n    21\tThe name `go` is intentionally familiar to users who know `core.async`, but the\n    22\timplementation is Dao-native. It must not use `core.async`, host threads,\n    23\tcallbacks, promises, or blocking calls as its core mechanism.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.await` is a syntax and process-construction layer. It is not `dao.flow`.\n    26\t`dao.flow` remains the datom coordination protocol over `dao.space`.\n    27\t\n    28\t## Homomorphism with `core.async`\n    29\t\n    30\tStructurally, `dao.await` is a *homomorphism* of `core.async`. It is a structure-preserving projection from the Clojure host domain into the Yin/Datom domain. The relationships between operations are preserved, allowing a developer's mental model to map perfectly from one to the other, even though the execution substrate is completely different:\n    31\t\n    32\t- **The Medium:** `core.async` channels map to `dao.stream`\n    33\t- **The Process:** `core.async/go` state machines map to Yin VM state maps\n    34\t- **The Read Operation:** Parking on a channel callback maps to Yin parking on a `:stream/next` effect\n    35\t- **The Write Operation:** Parking on a full channel maps to Yin parking on a `:stream/put` effect\n    36\t- **The Scheduler:** Host Thread Pool / Event Loop maps to the `dao.runtime` ready queue and its polling wait set\n    37\t\n    38\tBecause the structure is preserved, developers gain the proven ergonomics of Communicating Sequential Processes (CSP) without violating the system invariant that **runtime state must be data**. While `core.async` compiles code into an opaque host-level state machine, `dao.await` compiles code into pure datoms interpreted by the Yin VM, leaving the entire execution state explicitly queryable and serializable.\n    39\t\n    40\t## Layer Boundaries\n    41\t\n    42\tThe implementation must preserve these boundaries:\n    43\t\n    44\t- `dao.stream` exposes non-blocking operations — `cursor`, `next`,\n    45\t  `append!`, `close!` — each returning an outcome from its closed set. There\n    46\t  is no `drain-one!` and no readiness query.\n    47\t- `dao.runtime` schedules resumable task maps. It must remain unaware of\n    48\t  continuations and must not gain await-specific control flow.\n    49\t- `yin.vm` interprets semantic datoms, owns continuations, and handles parking\n    50\t  and resumption for stream effects.\n    51\t- `yang.clojure` compiles Clojure forms to Universal AST.\n    52\t- `dao.await` supplies user-facing syntax and starts or describes a Yin-backed\n    53\t  stream process.\n    54\t\n    55\tThe key invariant is:\n    56\t\n    57\t```text\n    58\tdao.await/go targets Universal AST, not dao.runtime.\n    59\t```\n    60\t\n    61\tAny continuation produced by an await block is a Yin VM continuation. It is not\n    62\ta `dao.await` continuation and not a `dao.runtime` continuation.\n    63\t\n    64\t## Public API\n    65\t\n    66\tV1 should add a `dao.await` namespace with these public forms or functions:\n    67\t\n    68\t```clojure\n    69\t(await/go body...)\n    70\t(await/cursor stream)\n    71\t(await/<! cursor)\n    72\t(await/>! stream value)\n    73\t```\n    74\t\n    75\tThe recommended v1 user shape is:\n   215\t### `await/<!`\n   216\t\n   217\t`await/<!` reads the next value from an explicit cursor.\n   218\t\n   219\t```clojure\n   220\t(await/<! c)\n   221\t```\n   222\t\n   223\tis equivalent to:\n   224\t\n   225\t```clojure\n   226\t(stream/next c)\n   227\t```\n   228\t\n   229\t(`dao.stream`'s read operation; `next` is also the operation a parked\n   230\treader is polled with.)\n   231\t\n   232\tIf the stream has a value at the cursor position, Yin returns that value and\n   233\tstores the successor cursor `next` returned. If the read returns `blocked`, Yin\n   234\tparks the current continuation using existing stream effect handling. Nothing\n   235\twakes the parked task: it resumes when the next poll of `dao.runtime`'s\n   236\twait set returns a value for its cursor. A value written after the park is\n   237\tseen by that poll, not by a wakeup from the write.\n   238\t\n   239\tOn stream end, v1 should preserve the existing Yin stream behavior. Do not\n   240\tinvent a new end-of-stream contract in `dao.await`.\n   241\t\n   242\t### `await/>!`\n   243\t\n   244\t`await/>!` writes a value to a stream.\n   245\t\n   246\t```clojure\n   247\t(await/>! out value)\n   248\t```\n   249\t\n   250\tis equivalent to the write operation of `dao.stream`, `append!` — the\n   251\tUniversal AST node stays `:stream/put`:\n   252\t\n   253\t```clojure\n   254\t(stream/append! out value)\n   255\t```\n   256\t\n   257\tIf the stream accepts the value, the expression returns the written value under\n   258\tthe existing stream effect behavior. If `append!` answers `full`, Yin parks the\n   259\tcurrent continuation. Nothing wakes it: `dao.runtime` re-attempts the append\n   260\ton every poll of the wait set, and the task resumes when a poll's append\n   261\tsucceeds. Over the v2 ring buffer `append!` never answers `full`, so parking a\n   262\twriter is reachable only over a transport that rejects.\n   263\t\n   264\t## Compilation Strategy\n   265\t\n   266\tV1 should avoid a second compiler. Reuse `yang.clojure` for form compilation.\n   267\t\n   268\tThere are two acceptable lowering routes. The implementation should choose the\n   269\troute that creates the least coupling after inspecting the current module\n   270\tresolver and Yang compiler paths. Do not add a new `:await/*` node family unless\n   405\t## Runtime Semantics\n   406\t\n   407\tThe await block runs as a Yin program.\n   408\t\n   409\tWhen it reaches a stream operation:\n   410\t\n   411\t- `await/cursor` creates a cursor value in the VM store.\n   412\t- `await/<!` executes the existing `:stream/next` effect.\n   413\t- `await/>!` executes the existing `:stream/put` effect.\n   414\t\n   415\tIf a stream operation can complete immediately, execution continues in the same\n   416\tVM run.\n   417\t\n   418\tIf a read is blocked or a write is full:\n   419\t\n   420\t- Yin parks the current continuation.\n   421\t- The park entry is wrapped as a `dao.runtime` task using the existing VM\n   422\t  adapter path.\n   423\t- `dao.runtime` records the task in the wait set. There are no\n   424\t  transport-local waiters: the polling wait set is the only mechanism.\n   425\t- Nothing wakes the task. Each poll resolves the entry against its transport —\n   426\t  `next` for a parked read, `append!` for a parked write — and a poll that\n   427\t  resolves it moves it to the ready queue. A stream that is written to,\n   428\t  drained, or closed wakes nothing: there are no drains, and `close!` returns\n   429\t  no wake list. A reader parked on a stream that is then closed learns of it\n   430\t  from its own next `next` answering `end`; a parked writer from `append!`\n   431\t  answering `closed`.\n   432\t- `dao.runtime` invokes the task's `:resume` function with the polled\n   433\t  outcome.\n   434\t- The VM restores the Yin continuation and continues evaluating the await body.\n   435\t\n   436\t`dao.await` must not implement these steps itself. It relies on the existing\n   437\tYin and runtime machinery.\n   438\t\n   439\t## Non-Goals\n   440\t\n   441\tV1 must not include:\n   442\t\n   443\t- `core.async` channels or go blocks.\n   444\t- Host thread or virtual-thread blocking semantics.\n   445\t- JVM-only `!!` operations as the primary behavior.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The plan’s high-level separation is promising, but I’m checking two pressure points closely: whether the proposed generic entry schema can actually preserve the VM’s current read/write semantics, and whether the W3 driver quietly reintroduces callbacks or mutable scheduling state above the stream boundary."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '230,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '1,220p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '220,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '1,310p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc | sed -n '1,180p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '230,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '1,220p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '220,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc | sed -n '1,310p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc | sed -n '1,180p'\"","aggregated_output":"   230\t            o (outcome result)\n   231\t            advance (fn [state* value]\n   232\t                      {:value value,\n   233\t                       :state (assoc state*\n   234\t                                     :store\n   235\t                                     (assoc store\n   236\t                                            cursor-id\n   237\t                                            (assoc cursor-data\n   238\t                                                   :cursor\n   239\t                                                   (:dao.stream/cursor\n   240\t                                                     result))))})]\n   241\t        (case o\n   242\t          :dao.stream/ok (advance state (:dao.stream/value result))\n   243\t          :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   244\t                               :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   245\t                               :stream-id stream-id,\n   246\t                               :state state}\n   247\t          :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :state state}\n   248\t          :dao.stream/gap (advance state :dao.stream/gap)\n   249\t          (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   250\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id, :cursor-id cursor-id}))))))\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\t(defn handle-close\n   254\t  \"Handle :stream/close. `close!` is total over {ok} and wakes nothing: a\n   255\t   reader parked on this stream learns of the close from its own next `next`.\n   256\t   Returns {:state s'}.\"\n   257\t  [state effect]\n   258\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   259\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   260\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   261\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   262\t    (stream/close! handle)\n   263\t    {:state state}))\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\t(defn- augment-wait-entry\n   267\t  \"Resolve one wait entry to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   268\t   store. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an earlier round\n   269\t   baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same cursor-ref may\n   270\t   have advanced the stored cursor since.\"\n   271\t  [store entry]\n   272\t  (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n   273\t    (let [cursor-data (get store (:id cursor-ref))]\n   274\t      (assoc entry\n   275\t             :stream (or (:stream entry)\n   276\t                         (get store (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n   277\t             :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)))\n   278\t    (if (and (not (:stream entry)) (:stream-id entry))\n   279\t      (assoc entry :stream (get store (:stream-id entry)))\n   280\t      entry)))\n   281\t\n   282\t\n   283\t(defn- poll-wait-entry\n   284\t  \"Poll one resolved wait entry against its transport, synchronously.\n   285\t\n   286\t   Returns nil while the entry must keep waiting — `blocked` for a reader,\n   287\t   `full` for a writer, the only two outcomes that can change on their own —\n   288\t   and otherwise the ready-entry updates `{:value :status}`, plus `:cursor`\n   289\t   for a reader that moved. `end` and `gap` resolve to the values the\n   290\t   immediate path yields; any other outcome resolves under its own keyword\n   291\t   for `resume-from-run-queue` to raise. A parked writer retries by\n   292\t   appending: the append is an effect of this poll. An entry with no known\n   293\t   `:reason` keeps waiting.\"\n   294\t  [entry]\n   295\t  (case (:reason entry)\n   296\t    :next (let [result (stream/next (:stream entry) (:cursor entry))\n   297\t                o (outcome result)]\n   298\t            (case o\n   299\t              :dao.stream/ok {:value (:dao.stream/value result),\n   300\t                              :status :ok,\n   301\t                              :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   302\t              :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   303\t              :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :status :end}\n   304\t              :dao.stream/gap {:value :dao.stream/gap,\n   305\t                               :status :dao.stream/gap,\n   306\t                               :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   307\t              {:value o, :status o}))\n   308\t    :put (let [o (outcome (stream/append! (:stream entry) (:datom entry)))]\n   309\t           (case o\n   310\t             :dao.stream/ok {:value (:datom entry), :status :ok}\n   311\t             :dao.stream/full nil\n   312\t             {:value o, :status o}))\n   313\t    nil))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn check-wait-set\n   317\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n   318\t\n   319\t   Every entry is resolved to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   320\t   store and polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution\n   321\t   happens per poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle.\n   322\t   There is no transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only\n   323\t   mechanism.\n   324\t\n   325\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   326\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   327\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values — the\n   328\t   value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next — instead\n   329\t   of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll cursor. An entry\n   330\t   that stays waiting is retained in its stored, resource-id form; the\n   331\t   resolved copy was for this poll only.\"\n   332\t  [state]\n   333\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   334\t    (if (empty? wait-set)\n   335\t      state\n   336\t      (loop [remaining wait-set\n   337\t             store (:store state)\n   338\t             waiting []\n   339\t             woken []]\n   340\t        (if (empty? remaining)\n   341\t          (let [v (assoc state\n   342\t                         :store store\n   343\t                         :wait-set waiting)]\n   344\t            (update v\n   345\t                    :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n   346\t                    (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken)))\n   347\t          (let [entry (first remaining)\n   348\t                augmented (augment-wait-entry store entry)]\n   349\t            (if-let [updates (poll-wait-entry augmented)]\n   350\t              (let [raw (merge augmented updates)\n   351\t                    cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref raw))\n   352\t                    ;; A woken reader's successor is stored before later\n   353\t                    ;; entries resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances\n   354\t                    ;; within the round.\n   355\t                    store (if (and cursor-id (:cursor raw))\n   356\t                            (assoc store\n   357\t                                   cursor-id\n   358\t                                   (assoc (get store cursor-id)\n   359\t                                          :cursor (:cursor raw)))\n   360\t                            store)]\n   361\t                (recur (rest remaining)\n   362\t                       store\n   363\t                       waiting\n   364\t                       (conj woken {:entry raw,\n   365\t                                    :value (:value raw),\n   366\t                                    :cursor (:cursor raw),\n   367\t                                    :store-updates (:store-updates raw)})))\n   368\t              (recur (rest remaining) store (conj waiting entry) woken))))))))\n   369\t\n   370\t\n   371\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n   372\t\n   373\t\n   374\t(defn run-loop\n   375\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n   376\t   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n   377\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n   378\t  (loop [v state]\n   379\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n   380\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n   381\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n   382\t                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n   383\t                              (recur resumed)\n   384\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n   385\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n   386\t                      (recur resumed)\n   387\t                      v)\n   388\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n   389\t\n   390\t\n   391\t(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n   392\t  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n   393\t   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` throw for every\n   394\t   outcome outside ok/end/gap, and whether the first attempt blocked must not\n   395\t   change that. An entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n   396\t  [entry]\n   397\t  (when (#{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n   398\t    (let [status (:status entry)]\n   399\t      (when-not (contains? #{nil :ok :end :dao.stream/gap} status) status))))\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\t(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n   403\t  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have.\"\n   404\t  [entry o]\n   405\t  (if (= :next (:reason entry))\n   406\t    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n   407\t          {:outcome o,\n   408\t           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n   409\t           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n   410\t    (fail \"Stream append failed\" {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream\n     2\t  \"DaoStream v2: passive substrate for IO in datom.world.\n     3\t\n     4\t   Seven operations:\n     5\t     Five handle operations (protocols implemented per declared surface):\n     6\t       descriptor (universal reachability and identity projection)\n     7\t       cursor     (reader surface)\n     8\t       next       (reader surface)\n     9\t       append!    (writer surface)\n    10\t       close!     (closable surface)\n    11\t     Two transport entry functions (per-transport, host-composed):\n    12\t       create!    (creates a new logical stream from a creation specification)\n    13\t       attach!    (attaches to an existing stream via portable descriptor)\n    14\t\n    15\t   Result Convention:\n    16\t     Every operation returns an open outcome map keyed by :dao.stream/outcome.\n    17\t     All qualified keywords live under :dao.stream/…\n    18\t     Each operation has an exhaustive, closed outcome set.\"\n    19\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [next]))\n    20\t\n    21\t\n    22\t;; =============================================================================\n    23\t;; The Seven Operations and Standard Keywords\n    24\t;; =============================================================================\n    25\t\n    26\t(def operations\n    27\t  \"The seven DaoStream v2 operations.\"\n    28\t  #{:create! :attach! :descriptor :cursor :next :append! :close!})\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(def surfaces\n    32\t  \"The public operational surfaces a handle may declare.\"\n    33\t  #{:reader :writer :closable})\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\t;; Anchors\n    37\t(def anchor-oldest\n    38\t  \"Earliest retained position on the stream.\"\n    39\t  :dao.stream/oldest)\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(def anchor-newest\n    43\t  \"Positioned after the newest appended value (observes next arrival).\"\n    44\t  :dao.stream/newest)\n    45\t\n    46\t\n    47\t(def standard-anchors\n    48\t  \"Contract-defined anchor keywords.\"\n    49\t  #{anchor-oldest anchor-newest})\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t;; Closed outcome sets per operation (contract exhaustive tables)\n    53\t(def outcomes-create\n    54\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    55\t    :dao.stream/invalid-spec\n    56\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    57\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(def outcomes-attach\n    61\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    62\t    :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor\n    63\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    64\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(def outcomes-descriptor\n    68\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(def outcomes-cursor\n    72\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    73\t    :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\n    74\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    75\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\t(def outcomes-next\n    79\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    80\t    :dao.stream/blocked\n    81\t    :dao.stream/end\n    82\t    :dao.stream/gap\n    83\t    :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n    84\t    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n    85\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    86\t\n    87\t\n    88\t(def outcomes-append\n    89\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    90\t    :dao.stream/full\n    91\t    :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    92\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    93\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(def outcomes-close\n    97\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(def operation-outcomes\n   101\t  \"Map of operation keyword to its closed outcome set.\"\n   102\t  {:create! outcomes-create\n   103\t   :attach! outcomes-attach\n   104\t   :descriptor outcomes-descriptor\n   105\t   :cursor outcomes-cursor\n   106\t   :next outcomes-next\n   107\t   :append! outcomes-append\n   108\t   :close! outcomes-close})\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\t(def outcome-required-keys\n   112\t  \"Map from [operation outcome] to required keys in the result map.\"\n   113\t  {[:create! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\n   114\t   [:attach! :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/handle}\n   115\t   [:descriptor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/descriptor :dao.stream/identity}\n   116\t   [:cursor :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/cursor}\n   117\t   [:next :dao.stream/ok] #{:dao.stream/value :dao.stream/cursor}\n   118\t   [:next :dao.stream/gap] #{:dao.stream/cursor}})\n   119\t\n   120\t\n   121\t;; =============================================================================\n   122\t;; Canonical Operation Resolution Helper\n   123\t;; =============================================================================\n   124\t\n   125\t(defn canonical-op\n   126\t  \"Normalizes op to canonical keyword (:create!, :attach!, :descriptor,\n   127\t   :cursor, :next, :append!, :close!). Supports aliases without '!'.\"\n   128\t  [op]\n   129\t  (case op\n   130\t    (:create :create!) :create!\n   131\t    (:attach :attach!) :attach!\n   132\t    (:descriptor) :descriptor\n   133\t    (:cursor) :cursor\n   134\t    (:next) :next\n   135\t    (:append :append!) :append!\n   136\t    (:close :close!) :close!\n   137\t    nil))\n   138\t\n   139\t\n   140\t;; =============================================================================\n   141\t;; Handle Protocols (Five Handle Operations)\n   142\t;; =============================================================================\n   143\t\n   144\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   145\t  \"Universal reachability and identity projection. Belongs to no surface:\n   146\t   implemented by every handle regardless of declared reader/writer/closable surfaces.\"\n   147\t\n   148\t  (descriptor\n   149\t    [handle]\n   150\t    \"Returns descriptor outcome map:\n   151\t     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   152\t      :dao.stream/descriptor <portable-descriptor>\n   153\t      :dao.stream/identity <logical-stream-identity>}\"))\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamReader\n   157\t  \"Reader surface: positioned, non-destructive observation via immutable cursors.\"\n   158\t\n   159\t  (cursor\n   160\t    [handle anchor]\n   161\t    \"Mint an immutable cursor from anchor (:dao.stream/oldest, :dao.stream/newest,\n   162\t     or transport-specific anchor).\n   163\t     Returns outcome map with :dao.stream/cursor on :dao.stream/ok.\")\n   164\t\n   165\t  (next\n   166\t    [handle cursor]\n   167\t    \"Read the value at cursor position. Total and non-blocking.\n   168\t     Returns read outcome map.\"))\n   169\t\n   170\t\n   171\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamWriter\n   172\t  \"Writer surface: append values to the sequence this handle's writer surface is on.\"\n   173\t\n   174\t  (append!\n   175\t    [handle val]\n   176\t    \"Append val to the sequence. Total and non-blocking.\n   177\t     Returns write outcome map.\"))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamClosable\n   181\t  \"Closable surface: close what this handle is on (logical stream or attachment).\n   182\t   Idempotent.\"\n   183\t\n   184\t  (close!\n   185\t    [handle]\n   186\t    \"Closes what this handle is on. Irrevocable.\n   187\t     Returns outcome map {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}.\"))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t;; =============================================================================\n   191\t;; Surface Inspection & Gating\n   192\t;; =============================================================================\n   193\t\n   194\t(defn reader?\n   195\t  \"True if handle implements the reader surface (IDaoStreamReader).\"\n   196\t  [handle]\n   197\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamReader handle))\n   198\t\n   199\t\n   200\t(defn writer?\n   201\t  \"True if handle implements the writer surface (IDaoStreamWriter).\"\n   202\t  [handle]\n   203\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamWriter handle))\n   204\t\n   205\t\n   206\t(defn closable?\n   207\t  \"True if handle implements the closable surface (IDaoStreamClosable).\"\n   208\t  [handle]\n   209\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamClosable handle))\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t(defn descriptor?\n   213\t  \"True if handle implements IDaoStreamDescriptor.\"\n   214\t  [handle]\n   215\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamDescriptor handle))\n   216\t\n   217\t\n   218\t(defn declared-surfaces\n   219\t  \"Returns set of public surfaces implemented by handle:\n   220\t   subset of #{:reader :writer :closable}.\"\n   220\t   subset of #{:reader :writer :closable}.\"\n   221\t  [handle]\n   222\t  (cond-> #{}\n   223\t    (reader? handle) (conj :reader)\n   224\t    (writer? handle) (conj :writer)\n   225\t    (closable? handle) (conj :closable)))\n   226\t\n   227\t\n   228\t;; =============================================================================\n   229\t;; Result Convention & Envelope Validation\n   230\t;; =============================================================================\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn qualified-keyword?*\n   233\t  \"True if x is a qualified keyword. Portable across CLJ, CLJS, CLJD.\"\n   234\t  [x]\n   235\t  (boolean (and (keyword? x) (namespace x))))\n   236\t\n   237\t\n   238\t(defn outcome-map?\n   239\t  \"True if x is an open outcome map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/outcome.\"\n   240\t  [x]\n   241\t  (and (map? x)\n   242\t       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/outcome x))))\n   243\t\n   244\t\n   245\t(defn validate-outcome\n   246\t  \"Validates an operation outcome against contract requirements.\n   247\t   Returns nil if valid, or a failure map describing the defect if invalid.\"\n   248\t  [op result]\n   249\t  (if-let [canon-op (canonical-op op)]\n   250\t    (cond\n   251\t      (not (outcome-map? result))\n   252\t      {:valid? false :error :invalid-outcome-map :operation canon-op :result result}\n   253\t\n   254\t      :else\n   255\t      (let [outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   256\t            allowed (get operation-outcomes canon-op)]\n   257\t        (cond\n   258\t          (not (contains? allowed outcome))\n   259\t          {:valid? false\n   260\t           :error :unauthorized-outcome\n   261\t           :operation canon-op\n   262\t           :outcome outcome\n   263\t           :allowed allowed}\n   264\t\n   265\t          :else\n   266\t          (let [req-keys (get outcome-required-keys [canon-op outcome] #{})\n   267\t                missing (into #{} (remove #(contains? result %) req-keys))]\n   268\t            (if (seq missing)\n   269\t              {:valid? false\n   270\t               :error :missing-required-keys\n   271\t               :operation canon-op\n   272\t               :outcome outcome\n   273\t               :missing missing}\n   274\t              nil)))))\n   275\t    {:valid? false :error :unknown-operation :operation op}))\n   276\t\n   277\t\n   278\t(defn valid-outcome?\n   279\t  \"True if result is a valid outcome map for op according to the contract.\"\n   280\t  [op result]\n   281\t  (nil? (validate-outcome op result)))\n   282\t\n   283\t\n   284\t(defn valid-envelope?\n   285\t  \"Generic envelope validation: envelope must be a map with a qualified keyword\n   286\t   under :dao.stream/type. Everything else is transport-owned.\"\n   287\t  [envelope]\n   288\t  (and (map? envelope)\n   289\t       (qualified-keyword?* (:dao.stream/type envelope))))\n   290\t\n   291\t\n   292\t(defn valid-creation-spec?\n   293\t  \"True if spec is a generically valid creation specification envelope:\n   294\t   must be a map with a qualified keyword under :dao.stream/type.\"\n   295\t  [spec]\n   296\t  (valid-envelope? spec))\n   297\t\n   298\t\n   299\t(defn valid-descriptor?\n   300\t  \"True if descriptor is a generically valid portable descriptor envelope:\n   301\t   must be a valid envelope (:dao.stream/type present and qualified)\n   302\t   and carry :dao.stream/identity.\"\n   303\t  [descriptor]\n   304\t  (and (valid-envelope? descriptor)\n   305\t       (contains? descriptor :dao.stream/identity)))\n   306\t\n   307\t\n   308\t(defn descriptor-identity-consistent?\n   309\t  \"True if a descriptor operation result is {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok ...}\n   310\t   where both :dao.stream/descriptor and :dao.stream/identity are present,\n   311\t   descriptor is a valid descriptor envelope, and the sibling :dao.stream/identity\n   312\t   projection structurally equals the identity inside the descriptor envelope.\"\n   313\t  [result]\n   314\t  (and (map? result)\n   315\t       (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   316\t       (contains? result :dao.stream/descriptor)\n   317\t       (contains? result :dao.stream/identity)\n   318\t       (let [sibling-id (:dao.stream/identity result)\n   319\t             desc (:dao.stream/descriptor result)]\n   320\t         (and (some? sibling-id)\n   321\t              (valid-descriptor? desc)\n   322\t              (= sibling-id (:dao.stream/identity desc))))))\n   323\t\n   324\t\n   325\t;; =============================================================================\n   326\t;; Open Result Map Constructors\n   327\t;; =============================================================================\n   328\t\n   329\t(defn ok-result\n   330\t  \"Convenience constructor for an open :dao.stream/ok outcome map.\"\n   331\t  ([]\n   332\t   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\n   333\t  ([extra-map]\n   334\t   (assoc extra-map :dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok)))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t(defn outcome-result\n   338\t  \"Convenience constructor for an open outcome map.\"\n   339\t  ([outcome]\n   340\t   {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\n   341\t  ([outcome extra-map]\n   342\t   (assoc extra-map :dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\n   343\t\n   344\t\n   345\t;; =============================================================================\n   346\t;; Transport Entry Functions & Host Dispatch\n   347\t;; =============================================================================\n   348\t;;\n   349\t;; Two transport entry functions: create! and attach!\n   350\t;; These take no handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions,\n   351\t;; host-composed, taking whatever composition state the transport needs.\n   352\t;;\n   353\t;; Putting create! or attach! on a protocol is how the retired registry grows back;\n   354\t;; DaoStream v2 ships no dispatch machinery (no multimethod, no registry,\n   355\t;; no load-time side effect).\n   356\t;;\n   357\t;; Dynamic dispatch on :dao.stream/type is performed by the host using an ordinary\n   358\t;; host-owned lookup table:\n   359\t;;\n   360\t;;   {:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n   361\t;;                            :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n   362\t;;    :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}\n   363\t;;\n   364\t;; The helper functions below provide transparent, purely functional host dispatch.\n   365\t\n   366\t(defn host-dispatch-create!\n   367\t  \"Host dispatch helper: looks up :dao.stream/create in dispatch-table\n   368\t   for (:dao.stream/type creation-spec).\n   369\t   Returns :dao.stream/invalid-spec if spec is not a valid envelope.\n   370\t   Returns :dao.stream/not-found if transport or :dao.stream/create is absent.\n   371\t   Otherwise invokes the transport's create function.\"\n   372\t  [dispatch-table creation-spec]\n   373\t  (if-not (valid-creation-spec? creation-spec)\n   374\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\n   375\t    (if-let [create-fn (get-in dispatch-table [(:dao.stream/type creation-spec) :dao.stream/create])]\n   376\t      (create-fn creation-spec)\n   377\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found})))\n   378\t\n   379\t\n   380\t(defn host-dispatch-attach!\n   381\t  \"Host dispatch helper: looks up :dao.stream/attach in dispatch-table\n   382\t   for (:dao.stream/type descriptor).\n   383\t   Returns :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor if descriptor is not a valid descriptor envelope.\n   384\t   Returns :dao.stream/not-found if transport or :dao.stream/attach is absent.\n   385\t   Otherwise invokes the transport's attach function.\"\n   386\t  [dispatch-table descriptor]\n   387\t  (if-not (valid-descriptor? descriptor)\n   388\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\n   389\t    (if-let [attach-fn (get-in dispatch-table [(:dao.stream/type descriptor) :dao.stream/attach])]\n   390\t      (attach-fn descriptor)\n   391\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found})))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.observer\n     2\t  \"Observer of one DaoStream medium: attachment, cursor, gap accounting, and\n     3\t   the coordination loop that feeds a consumer one batch at a time.\n     4\t\n     5\t   The medium exists independently of any consumer. Host composition supplies\n     6\t   a unary attach capability and a portable DaoStream descriptor; `attach`\n     7\t   calls that capability exactly once, validates the returned handle against\n     8\t   the reader surface, and mints the observer's cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\n     9\t   directly through `dao.stream` — or keeps a cursor handed to it, which is\n    10\t   how a session resumes from where it stopped. The observer never creates,\n    11\t   appends to, or closes the medium, and its complete initial state is\n    12\t   `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\n    13\t\n    14\t   This namespace requires only `dao.stream` and `dao.stream.observe`,\n    15\t   whose single `step` it loops over. It cannot depend on a ring buffer, a\n    16\t   WebSocket adapter, a resolver representation, a transport key or state, or\n    17\t   any consumer's namespace: transport-specific composition constructs the\n    18\t   unary attacher outside this boundary, for example by partially applying a\n    19\t   dispatch table or a transport resolver. The consumer is opaque here — a\n    20\t   `yin.vm` evaluator, a macro expander, a `dao.space` indexer, or anything\n    21\t   else that can be asked whether it is ready, handed a batch, and run.\n    22\t\n    23\t   A `gap` is recoverable here and only here in the generic machinery. A batch\n    24\t   the observer never saw is advanced past at the recovery cursor and counted,\n    25\t   and observation continues with the next retained batch; silently pretending\n    26\t   the medium was contiguous hides missing data. What a host does with a gap\n    27\t   count is host policy — the REPL shell, for one, reports the loss and\n    28\t   refuses further evaluation until reset — and that policy lives above this\n    29\t   boundary. Terminal read outcomes are errors.\n    30\t\n    31\t   Exports exactly `attach`, `observe-next`, and `run-on-stream`.\"\n    32\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    33\t            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]))\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\t;; =============================================================================\n    37\t;; Attachment\n    38\t;; =============================================================================\n    39\t\n    40\t(defn- attached-handle\n    41\t  \"Call the unary capability once and validate its answer, yielding the\n    42\t   handle both `attach` arities build on: a non-`ok` outcome and a handle\n    43\t   without the reader surface throw exactly as `attach` documents.\"\n    44\t  [attach! descriptor]\n    45\t  (let [result (attach! descriptor)\n    46\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    47\t    (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n    48\t      (throw (ex-info \"Stream attachment failed\"\n    49\t                      {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\n    50\t                       :descriptor descriptor,\n    51\t                       :result result}))\n    52\t      (let [handle (:dao.stream/handle result)]\n    53\t        (if-not (stream/reader? handle)\n    54\t          (throw (ex-info \"Attached handle declares no reader surface\"\n    55\t                          {:descriptor descriptor,\n    56\t                           :surfaces (stream/declared-surfaces handle)}))\n    57\t          handle)))))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn attach\n    61\t  \"Attach to an existing medium and return the initial observer state, or\n    62\t   throw.\n    63\t\n    64\t   `(attach attach! descriptor)` mints the cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\n    65\t   and returns `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\n    66\t   `(attach attach! descriptor {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g})` re-attaches at\n    67\t   a kept cursor, returning `{:stream handle :cursor c :ingress-gaps g}`\n    68\t   without minting one: the composition resuming a session knows where its\n    69\t   previous one stopped, a cursor already covers repositioning, the\n    70\t   transport validates the kept cursor on the first `next`, and the gap\n    71\t   count is seeded from the checkpoint rather than reset to zero.\n    72\t\n    73\t   `attach!` is a unary attachment capability: `(attach! descriptor)`. It is\n    74\t   called exactly once and there is no fallback to stream creation. A\n    75\t   non-`ok` attachment or cursor outcome throws `ex-info` preserving the\n    76\t   original `:dao.stream/outcome`. A handle without the reader surface is a\n    77\t   host assembly error reported with the descriptor and the handle's declared\n    78\t   surfaces, without inventing a DaoStream outcome. Attachment cleanup stays\n    79\t   with the host: this function closes nothing, and a failure leaves no\n    80\t   partial observer behind.\"\n    81\t  ([attach! descriptor]\n    82\t   (let [handle (attached-handle attach! descriptor)\n    83\t         minted (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\n    84\t         mint-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome minted)]\n    85\t     (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok mint-outcome)\n    86\t       (throw (ex-info \"Observer cursor could not be minted\"\n    87\t                       {:dao.stream/outcome mint-outcome,\n    88\t                        :descriptor descriptor,\n    89\t                        :result minted}))\n    90\t       {:stream handle,\n    91\t        :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted),\n    92\t        :ingress-gaps 0})))\n    93\t  ([attach! descriptor {:keys [cursor ingress-gaps]}]\n    94\t   {:stream (attached-handle attach! descriptor),\n    95\t    :cursor cursor,\n    96\t    :ingress-gaps ingress-gaps}))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t;; =============================================================================\n   100\t;; Observation\n   101\t;; =============================================================================\n   102\t\n   103\t(defn- terminal\n   104\t  \"Throw, preserving the original read outcome and the observer's own state.\"\n   105\t  [message outcome observer]\n   106\t  (throw (ex-info message\n   107\t                  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\n   108\t                   :cursor (:cursor observer),\n   109\t                   :ingress-gaps (:ingress-gaps observer)})))\n   110\t\n   111\t\n   112\t(def ^:private uncontinuable-read-outcomes\n   113\t  \"The contract's read outcomes this observer cannot continue from.\"\n   114\t  #{:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n   115\t    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   116\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn- answered-outcome\n   120\t  \"The outcome the transport actually answered with.\n   121\t\n   122\t   `observe/step` classifies an answer outside the contract as\n   123\t   `transport-error` and retains the raw answer, so the observer reports what\n   124\t   it was told rather than the classification.\"\n   125\t  [read]\n   126\t  (if (contains? read :dao.stream/answer)\n   127\t    (:dao.stream/outcome (:dao.stream/answer read))\n   128\t    (:dao.stream/outcome read)))\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\t(defn- observation-terminal\n   132\t  \"This observer's policy for a step defect: throw, naming the outcome the\n   133\t   transport answered with.\"\n   134\t  [observed observer]\n   135\t  (let [outcome (answered-outcome (:read observed))]\n   136\t    (if (contains? uncontinuable-read-outcomes outcome)\n   137\t      (terminal \"Observation cannot continue from this cursor\"\n   138\t                outcome\n   139\t                observer)\n   140\t      (terminal \"Unexpected DaoStream outcome while observing the medium\"\n   141\t                outcome\n   142\t                observer))))\n   143\t\n   144\t\n   145\t(defn- adopt-gap\n   146\t  \"This observer's gap policy: commit the recovery cursor and count the loss.\"\n   147\t  [observer observed]\n   148\t  (-> observer\n   149\t      (assoc :cursor (:recovery observed))\n   150\t      (update :ingress-gaps (fnil inc 0))))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn observe-next\n   154\t  \"Observe one batch from the attached medium.\n   155\t\n   156\t   Returns `{:status :ok :batch batch :observer observer'}` on `ok`, where the\n   157\t   successor state carries the exact returned cursor; `{:status :blocked\n   158\t   :observer observer}` and `{:status :end :observer observer}`, which retain\n   159\t   the cursor; or `{:status :gap :observer observer'}`, where the successor\n   160\t   carries the recovery cursor and one more counted `:ingress-gaps`. Terminal\n   161\t   outcomes (`cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`) and\n   162\t   unexpected ones throw with the original outcome preserved.\"\n   163\t  [observer]\n   164\t  (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   165\t                               (:cursor observer)\n   166\t                               (fn [_value] {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))]\n   167\t    (case (:status observed)\n   168\t      :advance {:status :ok,\n   169\t                :batch (:dao.stream/value (:read observed)),\n   170\t                :observer (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))}\n   171\t\n   172\t      :retry {:status :blocked, :observer observer}\n   173\t      :ended {:status :end, :observer observer}\n   174\t      :gap {:status :gap, :observer (adopt-gap observer observed)}\n   175\t\n   176\t      (observation-terminal observed observer))))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t;; =============================================================================\n   180\t;; Coordination\n   181\t;; =============================================================================\n   182\t\n   183\t(defn- consumer-left-by-run\n   184\t  \"The consumer state a failing `run` left, for the carried session.\n   185\t\n   186\t   A `run` that made partial progress before throwing — a flush that\n   187\t   delivered one medium and failed on another — reports the state it reached\n   188\t   by carrying a `:consumer` in its exception's data, so a retry resumes\n   189\t   from that progress rather than repeating delivered work. A `run` that\n   190\t   reports nothing left the state it was handed, which is the furthest\n   191\t   progress the coordination can name for it.\"\n   192\t  [e handed]\n   193\t  (let [data (ex-data e)]\n   194\t    (if (contains? data :consumer)\n   195\t      (:consumer data)\n   196\t      handed)))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t(defn- carry-session\n   200\t  \"Rethrow `e`, keeping the throw and carrying the partial session in the\n   201\t   exception's data.\n   202\t\n   203\t   The original message and data are preserved and `:session` is added. The\n   204\t   rethrow is a new `ExceptionInfo` whose cause is the original, so a caller\n   205\t   matching the original exception's concrete type no longer matches the\n   206\t   wrapper — `(ex-cause caught)` names it. Recovery is opt-in: a caller that\n   207\t   does not read `:session` keeps the old semantics — a throw means the\n   208\t   round failed — and a caller that catches resumes from the carried\n   209\t   session, which names the furthest progress the round had made where the\n   210\t   failure happened.\"\n   211\t  [session e]\n   212\t  (throw (ex-info (or (ex-message e) (str e))\n   213\t                  (assoc (ex-data e) :session session)\n   214\t                  e)))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn run-on-stream\n   218\t  \"Coordinate one `{:observer observer :consumer consumer}` session over the\n   219\t   attached medium, returning the updated session.\n   220\t\n   221\t   The consumer is any value the three supplied functions agree on. `ready?`\n   222\t   decides when it may accept another batch, `load` hands it one observed\n   223\t   batch and returns the successor consumer, and `run` lets it do whatever\n   224\t   work a loaded batch implies — evaluate, expand, index, forward — and\n   225\t   returns the successor. This is coordination, not interpretation: no field\n   226\t   of the consumer is inspected, so any consumer shape can be driven.\n   227\t\n   228\t   A consumer that is not ready runs first, and observation follows only if\n   229\t   it becomes ready. A ready consumer observes one batch; `gap` commits the\n   230\t   recovery cursor and continues; `blocked` and `end` return the session.\n   231\t   After a batch loads and runs, a consumer that is ready again observes the\n   232\t   next batch, and one that is not returns the session without another read.\n   233\t\n   234\t   A `load` or `run` that throws still propagates — the throw is kept — but\n   235\t   carries the partial session in the exception's data under `:session`, so\n   236\t   recovery is opt-in. Where the failure happened decides the carried\n   237\t   session: a throwing `load` carries the cursor *before* the failing batch,\n   238\t   so a retry re-reads it; a throwing `run` carries the cursor *after* the\n   239\t   loaded batch and the consumer the failing `run` left — the partial state\n   240\t   it reported under `:consumer` in its own exception's data when it made\n   241\t   progress before throwing, else the state `load` returned — so a retry\n   242\t   neither re-reads the batch nor repeats work already delivered; a\n   243\t   terminal read after processed batches carries the session those batches\n   244\t   produced. A consumer whose input can be bad should therefore return that\n   245\t   as data from `load` rather than throw, and reserve the throw for its own\n   246\t   defects.\"\n   247\t  [session ready? load run]\n   248\t  (loop [{:keys [observer consumer]} session]\n   249\t    (if-not (ready? consumer)\n   250\t      (let [consumer' (try (run consumer)\n   251\t                           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n   252\t                             (carry-session\n   253\t                               {:observer observer,\n   254\t                                :consumer (consumer-left-by-run e consumer)}\n   255\t                               e)))]\n   256\t        (if (ready? consumer')\n   257\t          (recur {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'})\n   258\t          {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'}))\n   259\t      ;; The load is the step's effect, so the cursor advances only once it has\n   260\t      ;; returned: a throwing load propagates before any successor exists, and\n   261\t      ;; the session it carries names the cursor before the failing batch.\n   262\t      (let [observed (try (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   263\t                                        (:cursor observer)\n   264\t                                        (fn [batch]\n   265\t                                          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\n   266\t                                           :dao.stream.observer/loaded\n   267\t                                           (load consumer batch)}))\n   268\t                          (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n   269\t                            (carry-session {:observer observer, :consumer consumer} e)))]\n   270\t        (case (:status observed)\n   271\t          :advance (let [loaded (:dao.stream.observer/loaded (:effect observed))\n   272\t                         ;; The batch was observed and loaded before the run, so\n   273\t                         ;; a throwing run carries the post-batch cursor and the\n   274\t                         ;; consumer it left: the partial state it reported in\n   275\t                         ;; its exception's data, else the loaded value.\n   276\t                         observer' (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))\n   277\t                         consumer' (try (run loaded)\n   278\t                                        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n   279\t                                          (carry-session\n   280\t                                            {:observer observer',\n   281\t                                             :consumer (consumer-left-by-run e loaded)}\n   282\t                                            e)))]\n   283\t                     (if (ready? consumer')\n   284\t                       (recur {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'})\n   285\t                       {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'}))\n   286\t          :gap (recur {:observer (adopt-gap observer observed),\n   287\t                       :consumer consumer})\n   288\t          ;; :retry (blocked) and :ended retain the cursor and end the round.\n   289\t          (:retry :ended) {:observer observer, :consumer consumer}\n   290\t          (try (observation-terminal observed observer)\n   291\t               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n   292\t                 (carry-session {:observer observer, :consumer consumer} e))))))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.forward\n     2\t  \"A single, host-agnostic interpreter step for copying one DaoStream to another.\n     3\t\n     4\t  `forward-step` owns no scheduler, callback, registry, or mutable state.  A\n     5\t  caller supplies the source cursor and calls the step again with the returned\n     6\t  state when its driver chooses.\"\n     7\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n     8\t            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]))\n     9\t\n    10\t\n    11\t(def default-options\n    12\t  \"Defaults for `forward-step`: one value per call and terminal gap policy.\"\n    13\t  {:batch-budget 1\n    14\t   :gap-policy :terminate})\n    15\t\n    16\t\n    17\t(defn initial-state\n    18\t  \"Create the state consumed by `forward-step` from a source cursor.\"\n    19\t  [cursor]\n    20\t  {:cursor cursor})\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\t(defn- result-state\n    24\t  [cursor status forwarded outcome]\n    25\t  (cond-> {:cursor cursor\n    26\t           :status status\n    27\t           :forwarded forwarded}\n    28\t    outcome (assoc :outcome outcome)))\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(defn- terminal-status\n    32\t  [side outcome]\n    33\t  ;; Keep dispatch scalar: ClojureDart cannot compile hashed vector constants\n    34\t  ;; in `case` expressions.\n    35\t  (if (= side :source)\n    36\t    (case outcome\n    37\t      :dao.stream/end :source-ended\n    38\t      :dao.stream/gap :source-gap\n    39\t      :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :source-cursor-mismatch\n    40\t      :dao.stream/invalid-cursor :source-invalid-cursor\n    41\t      :dao.stream/transport-error :source-transport-error\n    42\t      :transport-error)\n    43\t    (case outcome\n    44\t      :dao.stream/closed :destination-closed\n    45\t      :dao.stream/invalid-value :destination-invalid-value\n    46\t      :dao.stream/transport-error :destination-transport-error\n    47\t      :transport-error)))\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t(defn- gap-action\n    51\t  [policy gap-result]\n    52\t  (let [action (if (fn? policy) (policy gap-result) policy)]\n    53\t    (case action\n    54\t      :resume :resume\n    55\t      :terminate :terminate\n    56\t      :terminate)))\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\t(defn- positive-budget\n    60\t  [options]\n    61\t  (let [budget (:batch-budget options)]\n    62\t    (if (and (integer? budget) (not (neg? budget)))\n    63\t      budget\n    64\t      0)))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn forward-step\n    68\t  \"Copy at most `:batch-budget` values from source to destination.\n    69\t\n    70\t  `source` must provide the DaoStream reader surface and `destination` the\n    71\t  writer surface. `state` is normally produced by `initial-state` or an earlier\n    72\t  call and contains `:cursor`. Options are ordinary data:\n    73\t\n    74\t  * `:batch-budget` is a non-negative integer. A zero budget performs no read.\n    75\t  * `:gap-policy` is `:resume` (continue from the gap result's recovery\n    76\t    cursor), `:terminate`, or a function receiving the gap result and returning\n    77\t    one of those keywords. A resume forwards no value, so it is bounded\n    78\t    separately: at most `:batch-budget` resumes happen in one call, after which\n    79\t    the step returns `:continue` at the last recovery cursor. A recovery cursor\n    80\t    equal to the cursor just read is a transport fixed point rather than\n    81\t    progress, and terminates the step as `:source-gap`.\n    82\t\n    83\t  The returned map always has `:cursor`, `:status`, and `:forwarded`.  It also\n    84\t  has `:outcome` whenever the last source/destination operation produced one.\n    85\t  `:continue` and `:retry` are non-terminal statuses. `:retry` is returned for\n    86\t  source `blocked` and destination `full`, and neither advances the cursor.\n    87\t  All other operation outcomes are terminal and are preserved as `:outcome`.\n    88\t  In particular, `end` only stops this interpreter; it never closes either\n    89\t  handle.\"\n    90\t  ([source destination state]\n    91\t   (forward-step source destination state default-options))\n    92\t  ([source destination state options]\n    93\t   (let [state (if (map? state) state {})\n    94\t         options (merge default-options (or options {}))\n    95\t         cursor (:cursor state)\n    96\t         status (:status state)]\n    97\t     ;; Terminal state is data owned by the driver. Re-stepping it is a no-op,\n    98\t     ;; which makes accidental extra driver ticks harmless and idempotent.\n    99\t     (if (and status (not (contains? #{:continue :retry} status)))\n   100\t       state\n   101\t       (loop [cursor cursor\n   102\t              remaining (positive-budget options)\n   103\t              resumes (positive-budget options)\n   104\t              forwarded 0]\n   105\t         (if (zero? remaining)\n   106\t           (result-state cursor :continue forwarded nil)\n   107\t           (let [observed (observe/step source\n   108\t                                        cursor\n   109\t                                        (fn [value]\n   110\t                                          (stream/append! destination value)))\n   111\t                 outcome (:outcome observed)]\n   112\t             (case (:status observed)\n   113\t               ;; The cursor advanced only because the append answered ok;\n   114\t               ;; `observe/step` owns that ordering.\n   115\t               :advance\n   116\t               (recur (:cursor observed) (dec remaining) resumes (inc forwarded))\n   117\t\n   118\t               ;; Source `blocked` and destination `full` are the same answer\n   119\t               ;; to this interpreter: no progress, cursor kept, step again.\n   120\t               :retry\n   121\t               (result-state cursor :retry forwarded outcome)\n   122\t\n   123\t               :gap\n   124\t               (let [recovery (:recovery observed)]\n   125\t                 (cond\n   126\t                   ;; A recovery cursor that does not move cannot be observed\n   127\t                   ;; from, whatever the policy asks for.  Terminating keeps the\n   128\t                   ;; step total against a defective transport.\n   129\t                   (or (not= :resume\n   130\t                             (gap-action (:gap-policy options) (:read observed)))\n   131\t                       (= recovery cursor))\n   132\t                   (result-state cursor :source-gap forwarded outcome)\n   133\t\n   134\t                   (pos? resumes)\n   135\t                   (recur recovery remaining (dec resumes) forwarded)\n   136\t\n   137\t                   ;; The resume allowance is spent.  Yield the recovered cursor\n   138\t                   ;; so the driver keeps this step's progress and decides when\n   139\t                   ;; to step again.\n   140\t                   :else (result-state recovery :continue forwarded nil)))\n   141\t\n   142\t               ;; A destination answer that is neither ok nor full.\n   143\t               :failed\n   144\t               (result-state cursor\n   145\t                             (terminal-status :destination outcome)\n   146\t                             forwarded\n   147\t                             outcome)\n   148\t\n   149\t               ;; end and every source-side failure are terminal. No close is\n   150\t               ;; implied: the composition decides transport-specific teardown.\n   151\t               (result-state cursor\n   152\t                             (terminal-status :source outcome)\n   153\t                             forwarded\n   154\t                             outcome)))))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
