{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '8,105p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '45,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '230,380p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '132,155p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '283,369p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.await.md | sed -n '350,440p'; date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; TZ=Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z'\"","aggregated_output":"     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    56\tthe stream's own.\n    57\t\n    58\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    59\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    60\t\n    61\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    62\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Host Boundaries\n    65\t\n    66\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    67\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    68\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    71\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    72\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    73\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    74\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    75\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    76\t\n    77\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    78\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    79\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    80\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    81\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    84\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    85\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    86\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    87\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    88\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    89\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    90\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    91\tnot an upward callback.\n    92\t\n    93\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    94\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    95\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    96\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n    97\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n    98\tfor facts it owns.\n    99\t\n   100\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   101\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   102\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   103\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   104\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   105\t\n    45\t## Strategy\n    46\t\n    47\tThree moves, each verifiable against a suite that already exists:\n    48\t\n    49\t1. **Extract the sweep into `dao.stream.waitset`**, a pure `.cljc` namespace\n    50\t   requiring only `dao.stream`. Entries are data, a host-composed resolver\n    51\t   maps stored references to live handles and cursors, and the sweep\n    52\t   partitions waiting from woken. No `:resume`, no ready queue, no task\n    53\t   semantics: the library multiplexes readiness; it does not schedule work.\n    54\t2. **Make `yin.vm.engine` its first consumer.** The engine supplies a\n    55\t   store-resolver and a woken-to-ready-queue disposition and keeps its\n    56\t   machine relation; the library owns the poll loop. The inline\n    57\t   `augment-wait-entry`/`poll-wait-entry`/`check-wait-set` are deleted, not\n    58\t   wrapped.\n    59\t3. **Restore cadence as `dao.stream.waitset.driver`**, one namespace name\n    60\t   and three host files, carrying over the deleted R2 drivers' settled\n    61\t   shape — explicit host-owned state, configurable interval, no\n    62\t   namespace-global atoms — plus one new entry: a `nudge!` that an\n    63\t   appending composition calls so an idle driver wakes immediately instead\n    64\t   of at the next tick.\n    65\t\n    66\tThe substrate is untouched in every phase. No transport changes, no\n    67\treadiness protocol, no wake tokens: `blocked` and `full` remain the only\n    68\twaitable outcomes, discovered by polling, exactly as the contract's *The\n    69\treadiness extension* requires (\"no consumer of this contract may depend on\n    70\t[a readiness extension] existing\").\n    71\t\n    72\t## What `dao.stream.waitset` is\n    73\t\n    74\tStated so no phase has to infer it from the engine's code.\n    75\t\n    76\t**State is a value.** `{:waiting [] :woken []}` from an initial empty set,\n    77\tor the host's own map threaded through. Every function takes the state and\n    78\treturns it. No atom, no `defonce`, no host branch in the pure namespace; it\n    79\trequires only `dao.stream`.\n    80\t\n    81\t**An entry is data with a reference, never a handle.** An entry is\n    82\t`{:reason :next|:put, :stream-ref …, :cursor-ref …}` — references the host\n    83\tunderstands, stored exactly as the host gave them. This is the engine's own\n    84\tdiscipline, kept: \"the stored entry itself never carries a handle\"\n    85\t(`engine.cljc:320-321`). Only two reasons exist, because only two outcomes\n    86\tcan change on their own: `blocked` for a reader, `full` for a writer\n    87\t(`engine.cljc:286-293`).\n    88\t\n    89\t**A resolver is host composition.** `(resolve-ref store ref) → {:stream\n    90\thandle :cursor cursor}` or nil. The VM's `:store` lookup is one\n    91\timplementation (`augment-wait-entry` becomes it almost verbatim); a test\n    92\tfake, a host directory, or a constant map are others. The library never\n    93\tdereferences a ref itself.\n    94\t\n    95\t**The sweep is one ordered pass.** `check` resolves each entry through the\n    96\tresolver, polls it with a synchronous `next` or `append!`, and partitions:\n    97\tstill-waiting entries are retained in stored form; resolved entries move to\n    98\t`:woken` with `{:value :status …}` updates plus the successor `:cursor` for\n    99\ta moved reader. Two properties are preserved verbatim from the engine,\n   100\tbecause suites depend on them:\n   101\t\n   102\t- **Shared-cursor write-back between entries.** When two entries share a\n   103\t  cursor-ref, the first woken reader's successor is folded into the\n   104\t  resolver's store before the second entry resolves, so co-waiters read\n   105\t  distinct values (`engine.cljc:325-331,350-356`). The sweep therefore\n   106\t  takes a store-updating callback… no: it takes a *resolver* and returns a\n   107\t  *new store*, `(check waitset resolver store) → {:waiting … :woken …\n   108\t  :store store'}` — pure in, pure out, write-back expressed as data.\n   109\t- **A parked writer retries by appending.** The append is an effect of the\n   110\t  poll; `check` returns the state that records it, and no caller may drop\n   111\t  a polled state (the old R1 defect, restated as a law).\n   112\t\n   113\t**Classification is total and declaration-driven.** `ok` resolves with the\n   114\tvalue and successor; `end` resolves as `{:value nil :status :end}`;\n   115\t`gap` resolves with its recovery cursor; `closed` resolves as `:end` (the\n   116\twriter-side twin); the terminal outcomes (`cursor-mismatch`,\n   117\t`invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`, `invalid-value`) resolve under their\n   118\town keywords for the host to raise or report. Only `blocked` and `full`\n   119\twait. An outcome outside the contract's closed sets is terminal, never a\n   120\twait — waiting on an answer the library cannot interpret would spin.\n   121\t\n   122\t**Woken entries are returned, never dispatched.** The library invokes\n   123\tnothing: no callbacks, no continuations, no queues of its own. What the\n   124\thost does with `:woken` — feed a ready queue, resolve a promise, run a\n   125\tconsumer function — is host disposition. This is the invariant \"do not\n   126\tintroduce callbacks\" held at the seam.\n   127\t\n   128\t**`check` answers whether anything moved.** Its result includes whether\n   129\t`:woken` is non-empty, because that single bit is what a driver sleeps on.\n   130\t\n   131\t## Decisions\n   132\t\n   133\tSettled here so no phase decides them alone.\n   134\t\n   135\t**The name is `dao.stream.waitset`.** `dao.runtime` was considered and\n   136\trejected: it was deleted one commit before this plan (`a99f246f`), the\n   137\tlibrary deliberately has none of what made the runtime a runtime (no\n   138\t`:resume`, no ready queue, no tasks), and \"name things after what they do\n   139\tto streams\" puts it beside `dao.stream.forward` and `dao.stream.observer`\n   140\tas another interpreter over the medium. The stale prose that names\n   141\t\"`dao.runtime`'s wait set\" (the `dao.stream.observe` namespace docstring)\n   142\tis repaired in W5 to point here.\n   143\t\n   144\t**The resolver seam is adopted now.** The runtime plan refused it for a\n   145\tsingle consumer; the census in W0 is the evidence a second consumer class\n   146\texists. Adopting it re-opens no review: the engine's semantics are\n   147\tpreserved branch-for-branch, and W2's deliverable is that the engine's\n   148\tsuite passes *unchanged*.\n   149\t\n   150\t**No shared queue, no fairness, no priorities, no wait-set index.** The\n   151\tsweep is in wait-set order and O(n) in parked entries, which the\n   152\tcontract's *What it costs* accepts and which W3's budget bounds per round.\n   153\tA priority structure would make `check` allocate and reorder for consumers\n   154\tthat park dozens of entries, not thousands. If a composition parks\n   155\tthousands, that composition needs the readiness extension, not a smarter\n   156\tlibrary.\n   157\t\n   158\t**The nudge is a host-to-library call, never the reverse.** `nudge!` is a\n   159\tfunction on the driver container that an *appending composition* calls\n   160\tafter it deposits — the ws inbound path after `deposit!`, a GUI event\n   161\thandler after its append, a test after a scripted append. The library\n   162\tnever registers anything with a transport, and the substrate still wakes\n   163\tnothing: `append!`'s outcome set is unchanged. Correctness never depends\n   164\ton the nudge — a lost nudge costs one tick of latency, never lost work,\n   165\tbecause the poll is the only truth. The nudge is the self-pipe trick with\n   166\tthe log playing the pipe's role: the data is already there; the nudge only\n   167\tends the sleep early.\n   168\t\n   169\t**Cadence parameters live on the container.** `:poll-ms` (the idle round\n   170\tinterval), `:budget` (max entries polled per round, bounding latency\n   171\tspikes), and `:backoff` (optional idle curve from `:poll-ms` up to a\n   172\tceiling, reset by any wake). The right cadence for a REPL is not the right\n   173\tcadence for a serving endpoint; neither is the library's to know.\n   174\t\n   175\t**Fixtures are v2 ring buffers or reified handles.** Where a test needs an\n   176\toutcome the ring buffer never produces — `full`, `transport-error`,\n   177\t`invalid-value` — a reified handle returns the scripted outcome, the same\n   178\trule the runtime plan set. No v1 fixture returns from the deleted test\n   179\ttree.\n   180\t\n   230\t## Phase W1 — The sweep\n   231\t\n   232\t`src/cljc/dao/stream/waitset.cljc`, pure, requiring only `dao.stream`:\n   233\t\n   234\t- `empty-waitset`, `park` (add an entry), `check` as specified above.\n   235\t- Tests iterate `dao.stream/outcomes-next` and `outcomes-append` and pin\n   236\t  the classification of every declared outcome — exactly `blocked` and\n   237\t  `full` wait — so a future contract outcome fails this suite instead of\n   238\t  falling into a default branch. A keyword outside the declared sets is\n   239\t  asserted terminal.\n   240\t- Tests for the two preserved properties: shared cursor-ref wakes\n   241\t  co-waiters on distinct values in one round; a scripted `full`-once\n   242\t  handle shows exactly one append per poll with the resolved writer in\n   243\t  `:woken` and no polled state discarded.\n   244\t\n   245\tDeliverable: suite green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd.\n   246\t\n   247\t## Phase W2 — The engine becomes a consumer\n   248\t\n   249\tReplace `augment-wait-entry`, `poll-wait-entry`, and `check-wait-set` in\n   250\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` with `waitset/check` plus a store-resolver\n   251\tand the woken-to-ready-queue disposition (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`\n   252\tstays the engine's). The run loop, park semantics, and terminal-outcome\n   253\traising are unchanged.\n   254\t\n   255\tDeliverable: the full `yin.vm` suite — engine, walker, FFI, macro, parity —\n   256\tgreen on all three hosts **without test edits**, and `grep` confirming the\n   257\tthree inline functions are gone. Any test that had to change is a defect in\n   258\tW1, not in the test.\n   259\t\n   260\t## Phase W3 — Cadence containers (`dao.stream.waitset.driver`)\n   261\t\n   262\tOne namespace name, three host files, the R2 shape with explicit state:\n   263\t`src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`,\n   264\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs`,\n   265\t`src/cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd`.\n   266\t\n   267\t- **clj**: `make-driver` returns `{:waitset … :queue (LinkedBlockingQueue.)\n   268\t  :poll-ms … :budget …}` held in an atom the host creates. `run-loop!`\n   269\t  blocks on the queue with the `:poll-ms` timeout — external entries and\n   270\t  `nudge!` wakes it immediately, timeout means the wait set is due a\n   271\t  round — then sweeps and dispatches `:woken` through the host's\n   272\t  disposition. This is `select` with the wakeup pipe; the sweep is the\n   273\t  syscall.\n   274\t- **cljs/cljd**: `schedule-work!` enqueues and schedules one microtask;\n   275\t  `run-pending!` sweeps and, if the wait set is non-empty, arms exactly\n   276\t  one timer at `:poll-ms` (cancel-and-rearm; Node timers `unref`ed), so an\n   277\t  idle composition costs one timer, zero busy loops.\n   278\t- `nudge!` on every host: enqueues a wake token (clj) or schedules the\n   279\t  pending run immediately (cljs/cljd).\n   280\t- Tests port the deleted R2 driver tests' intents over v2 ring buffers and\n   281\t  a host-created container: internal work drains, a parked wait set keeps\n   282\t  being polled without external work, `nudge!` beats the timer, repeated\n   283\t  nudges coalesce, an idle driver stays alive at backoff ceiling.\n   284\t\n   285\tDeliverable: three drivers and suites on their hosts. No production\n   286\tconsumer is switched in this phase, and the plan says so rather than\n   287\tinventing one: the drivers exist because a sweep without cadence has no\n   288\tanswer to \"what calls `check` again\" on any host, and because `a99f246f`\n   289\tdeleted the only answer that existed.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Phase W4 — Consumer adoption\n   292\t\n   293\tMigrate the multiplexed consumers W0 identified — expected: the GUI event\n   294\tpump and `dao.stream.rpc`'s serving loop; `dao.jing` if its materializer\n   295\tholds more than one waiter. Single-stream consumers stay on `observe/step`\n   296\tand `observer/run-on-stream`: the waitset is for hosts holding many\n   297\twaiters, and converting a one-stream loop onto it is the over-adoption\n   298\tfailure mode this phase exists to avoid. Each adoption deletes the loop it\n   299\treplaces; a consumer adopted without deleting anything has been wrapped,\n   300\tnot migrated, and is reverted.\n   301\t\n   302\tDeliverable: each adopted consumer's suite green on all three hosts with\n   303\tits private poll loop gone.\n   304\t\n   305\t## Phase W5 — Design prose and stale references\n   306\t\n   307\t- The `dao.stream.observe` namespace docstring's reference to\n   308\t  \"`dao.runtime`'s wait set\" names `dao.stream.waitset`.\n   309\t- [`dao.await.md`](./dao.await.md) describes resumption as \"the scheduler\n   310\t  polls each parked entry\" — the sentence gains the library's name and the\n   311\t  nudge rule (the VM parks; the library sweeps; the driver keeps cadence).\n   312\t- [`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) *The readiness extension* gains one\n   313\t  sentence pointing at this library as the standing answer to *What it\n   314\t  costs*: poll-based, substrate-untouched, and the reason no consumer\n   315\t  needs the reserved extension to sleep between rounds.\n   316\t\n   317\tDeliverable: the three docs updated; no other prose touched.\n   318\t\n   319\t## Divergence register\n   320\t\n   321\tEvery place the library deliberately differs from what it replaces, with\n   322\tthe reason.\n   323\t\n   324\t| Prior form | `dao.stream.waitset` | Why |\n   325\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   326\t| Engine entries carry VM registers and ride the machine relation | Entries are `{:reason :stream-ref :cursor-ref}` data; disposition is host-owned | A VM entry is a continuation; the library multiplexes readiness. Register-bearing entries would drag the VM into every consumer |\n   327\t| Engine resolves refs against its `:store` internally (`engine.cljc:266`) | Resolver is a parameter; `check` returns a new store | The deferred decision, adopted: a second consumer class appeared (W0) |\n   328\t| `dao.runtime` had `:resume`, a ready queue, and task semantics | None of the three | Task scheduling is why the old runtime was coupled to its consumers; readiness multiplexing is the reusable core. A library with `:resume` is a scheduler, and schedulers are per-host policy |\n   329\t| Deleted R2 drivers polled on a fixed timer only | `nudge!` wakes an idle driver immediately | The timer-only shape pays full `:poll-ms` latency on externally-appended data; the nudge halves the cost of the select analogy without touching the substrate |\n   330\t| `run-once`/`run-loop` combined ready work and polling | `check` polls; the host's disposition runs woken work | The R1 split, restated: a polled state is never discarded, and the sweep owns no downstream work to drop |\n   331\t| Unknown wait `:reason` kept waiting (old runtime default) | Unchanged | An entry the library cannot poll is not the library's to resolve; it stays where the host put it |\n   332\t\n   333\t## Host matrix\n   334\t\n   335\tW1 and W2 are pure `.cljc` over `dao.stream` and have no host branch. W3 is\n   336\tthree host files by construction. Every phase green on clj, cljs (Node) and\n   337\tcljd; per the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.stream.waitset-test` and\n   338\t`Testing dao.stream.waitset.driver-test` appear in the Node output rather\n   339\tthan assuming discovery. The cljd test lane regenerates `test/cljd-out/`;\n   340\tonly one process may own it at a time.\n   341\t\n   342\t## Boundary of this plan\n   343\t\n   344\t**Not in this plan, by design:**\n   345\t\n   346\t- Any change to `dao.stream`, its protocols, outcome sets, or transports.\n   347\t  Zero diff is an end condition.\n   348\t- The contract's reserved readiness extension. The library is the\n   349\t  demonstration that polling plus a nudge suffices; if it ever is not,\n   350\t  the extension lands in transports and this library consumes it —\n   351\t  additively, per the contract.\n   352\t- `dao.await` and the VM's park/resume relation. W2 swaps the engine's\n   353\t  sweep implementation only.\n   354\t- Fairness, priorities, indexes (see *Decisions*), and any queue\n   355\t  interpreter restoring destructive take — an interpreter above the\n   356\t  stream, per the runtime plan's boundary, not a wait-set feature.\n   357\t- Consumers that stay single-stream on `observe/step`. They are not\n   358\t  technical debt; they are the correct shape for one stream and one\n   359\t  consumer.\n   360\t\n   361\t## End condition\n   362\t\n   363\tComplete when all of the following hold on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd:\n   364\t\n   365\t- `dao.stream.waitset` requires only `dao.stream`, classifies every\n   366\t  declared outcome with exactly `blocked`/`full` waiting, preserves the\n   367\t  shared-cursor and no-dropped-poll laws, and its suite proves each by\n   368\t  construction.\n   369\t- `yin.vm.engine` contains no inline wait-set functions and its suites\n   370\t  pass unmodified.\n   371\t- `dao.stream.waitset.driver` exists per host with explicit state,\n   372\t  configurable cadence, budget, and `nudge!`, over v2 fixtures only.\n   373\t- The W4 consumers run the library and contain no private multiplexed\n   374\t  poll loops; the single-stream consumers are recorded as deliberately\n   375\t  unchanged in W0's table.\n   376\t- `git diff <base> -- src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/`\n   377\t  shows no transport changes beyond the new `waitset/` subtree.\n   378\t- The W5 prose names the library, and no doc or docstring names the\n   379\t  deleted `dao.runtime` as a live namespace.\n   132\t(defn make-woken-run-queue-entries\n   133\t  \"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\n   134\t   Readers (with :cursor-ref) store the successor cursor the transport\n   135\t   returned. Writers (no :cursor-ref) just stamp :value.\n   136\t\n   137\t   The ready entry stays pure data: the `:stream` handle the poll resolved\n   138\t   is dropped — the store-updates carry the successor cursor, and handles\n   139\t   are re-resolved from ids when needed again — so an entry neither waits\n   140\t   nor runs holding a host object.\"\n   141\t  [state woken]\n   142\t  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor], :as woken-entry}]\n   143\t          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\n   144\t                store-updates\n   145\t                (or (:store-updates woken-entry)\n   146\t                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\n   147\t                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   148\t                            cursor-data (get (:store state) cursor-id)]\n   149\t                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\n   150\t            (dissoc (assoc entry\n   151\t                           :value value\n   152\t                           :store-updates store-updates\n   153\t                           :cursor cursor)\n   154\t                    :stream)))\n   155\t        woken))\n   283\t(defn- poll-wait-entry\n   284\t  \"Poll one resolved wait entry against its transport, synchronously.\n   285\t\n   286\t   Returns nil while the entry must keep waiting — `blocked` for a reader,\n   287\t   `full` for a writer, the only two outcomes that can change on their own —\n   288\t   and otherwise the ready-entry updates `{:value :status}`, plus `:cursor`\n   289\t   for a reader that moved. `end` and `gap` resolve to the values the\n   290\t   immediate path yields; any other outcome resolves under its own keyword\n   291\t   for `resume-from-run-queue` to raise. A parked writer retries by\n   292\t   appending: the append is an effect of this poll. An entry with no known\n   293\t   `:reason` keeps waiting.\"\n   294\t  [entry]\n   295\t  (case (:reason entry)\n   296\t    :next (let [result (stream/next (:stream entry) (:cursor entry))\n   297\t                o (outcome result)]\n   298\t            (case o\n   299\t              :dao.stream/ok {:value (:dao.stream/value result),\n   300\t                              :status :ok,\n   301\t                              :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   302\t              :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   303\t              :dao.stream/end {:value nil, :status :end}\n   304\t              :dao.stream/gap {:value :dao.stream/gap,\n   305\t                               :status :dao.stream/gap,\n   306\t                               :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor result)}\n   307\t              {:value o, :status o}))\n   308\t    :put (let [o (outcome (stream/append! (:stream entry) (:datom entry)))]\n   309\t           (case o\n   310\t             :dao.stream/ok {:value (:datom entry), :status :ok}\n   311\t             :dao.stream/full nil\n   312\t             {:value o, :status o}))\n   313\t    nil))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn check-wait-set\n   317\t  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n   318\t\n   319\t   Every entry is resolved to a live handle and an opaque cursor out of the\n   320\t   store and polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution\n   321\t   happens per poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle.\n   322\t   There is no transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only\n   323\t   mechanism.\n   324\t\n   325\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   326\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   327\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values — the\n   328\t   value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next — instead\n   329\t   of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll cursor. An entry\n   330\t   that stays waiting is retained in its stored, resource-id form; the\n   331\t   resolved copy was for this poll only.\"\n   332\t  [state]\n   333\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   334\t    (if (empty? wait-set)\n   335\t      state\n   336\t      (loop [remaining wait-set\n   337\t             store (:store state)\n   338\t             waiting []\n   339\t             woken []]\n   340\t        (if (empty? remaining)\n   341\t          (let [v (assoc state\n   342\t                         :store store\n   343\t                         :wait-set waiting)]\n   344\t            (update v\n   345\t                    :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n   346\t                    (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken)))\n   347\t          (let [entry (first remaining)\n   348\t                augmented (augment-wait-entry store entry)]\n   349\t            (if-let [updates (poll-wait-entry augmented)]\n   350\t              (let [raw (merge augmented updates)\n   351\t                    cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref raw))\n   352\t                    ;; A woken reader's successor is stored before later\n   353\t                    ;; entries resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances\n   354\t                    ;; within the round.\n   355\t                    store (if (and cursor-id (:cursor raw))\n   356\t                            (assoc store\n   357\t                                   cursor-id\n   358\t                                   (assoc (get store cursor-id)\n   359\t                                          :cursor (:cursor raw)))\n   360\t                            store)]\n   361\t                (recur (rest remaining)\n   362\t                       store\n   363\t                       waiting\n   364\t                       (conj woken {:entry raw,\n   365\t                                    :value (:value raw),\n   366\t                                    :cursor (:cursor raw),\n   367\t                                    :store-updates (:store-updates raw)})))\n   368\t              (recur (rest remaining) store (conj waiting entry) woken))))))))\n   369\t\n   350\talready has `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, and `:stream/put` node types.\n   351\t\n   352\tThe implementation decision should be documented in the first patch that adds\n   353\t`dao.await`, with a short note explaining why the selected route has less\n   354\tcoupling in the current codebase.\n   355\t\n   356\t## Process Construction\n   357\t\n   358\t`await/go` must produce or start a Yin-backed process from the compiled AST.\n   359\t\n   360\tAn implementation agent should first inspect the current VM constructors and\n   361\ttest helpers. Existing paths include:\n   362\t\n   363\t- `yin.vm/eval`, which compiles AST through `ast->datoms`.\n   364\t- VM-specific load-program functions in the AST walker, stack VM, register VM,\n   365\t  and semantic VM.\n   366\t- `dao.runtime` task maps with a `:resume` function.\n   367\t\n   368\tV1 should choose the smallest integration that allows tests to drive an await\n   369\tprogram through an existing VM implementation. Avoid adding a global default VM\n   370\tor global scheduler.\n   371\t\n   372\tA reasonable v1 shape is:\n   373\t\n   374\t```clojure\n   375\t(defn go*\n   376\t  [{:keys [env ast datoms forms] :as opts}]\n   377\t  ;; 1. prefer precompiled ast/datoms supplied by the macro\n   378\t  ;; 2. if absent, compile `(do ~@forms)` with yang.clojure and cache it\n   379\t  ;; 3. seed captured env into the Yin execution context\n   380\t  ;; 4. load datoms into the VM supplied in opts, or return a process descriptor\n   381\t  ;; 5. leave scheduling to the caller or explicit runtime driver\n   382\t  )\n   383\t```\n   384\t\n   385\tThe macro form can wrap this:\n   386\t\n   387\t```clojure\n   388\t(defmacro go\n   389\t  [& body]\n   390\t  ;; Conceptual only:\n   391\t  ;; - compute free symbols in body\n   392\t  ;; - compile body to AST/datoms if possible\n   393\t  ;; - emit runtime captures for free symbols\n   394\t  `(go* {:env <captured-env>\n   395\t         :ast <precompiled-ast>\n   396\t         :datoms <optional-precompiled-datoms>\n   397\t         :forms '~body}))\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\tThis is illustrative, not final code. The implementation must account for CLJC\n   401\tmacro constraints and the existing compiler API. The important point is that\n   402\tthe source forms are compiled by Yang, not interpreted by `dao.await`, and host\n   403\tlexical values are represented explicitly.\n   404\t\n   405\t## Runtime Semantics\n   406\t\n   407\tThe await block runs as a Yin program.\n   408\t\n   409\tWhen it reaches a stream operation:\n   410\t\n   411\t- `await/cursor` creates a cursor value in the VM store.\n   412\t- `await/<!` executes the existing `:stream/next` effect.\n   413\t- `await/>!` executes the existing `:stream/put` effect.\n   414\t\n   415\tIf a stream operation can complete immediately, execution continues in the same\n   416\tVM run.\n   417\t\n   418\tIf a read is blocked or a write is full:\n   419\t\n   420\t- Yin parks the current continuation.\n   421\t- The park entry is wrapped as a `dao.runtime` task using the existing VM\n   422\t  adapter path.\n   423\t- `dao.runtime` records the task in the wait set. There are no\n   424\t  transport-local waiters: the polling wait set is the only mechanism.\n   425\t- Nothing wakes the task. Each poll resolves the entry against its transport —\n   426\t  `next` for a parked read, `append!` for a parked write — and a poll that\n   427\t  resolves it moves it to the ready queue. A stream that is written to,\n   428\t  drained, or closed wakes nothing: there are no drains, and `close!` returns\n   429\t  no wake list. A reader parked on a stream that is then closed learns of it\n   430\t  from its own next `next` answering `end`; a parked writer from `append!`\n   431\t  answering `closed`.\n   432\t- `dao.runtime` invokes the task's `:resume` function with the polled\n   433\t  outcome.\n   434\t- The VM restores the Yin continuation and continues evaluating the await body.\n   435\t\n   436\t`dao.await` must not implement these steps itself. It relies on the existing\n   437\tYin and runtime machinery.\n   438\t\n   439\t## Non-Goals\n   440\t\n2026-09-19 17:53:37 GMT\n2026-09-20 00:53:37 +07\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
